window.NIFAT32_DOCS = {"docs/getting-started":{"title":"Getting started","group":"Start here","source":"docs/getting-started.md","html":"<h1 id=\"getting-started\">Getting started</h1>\n<p>The root Makefile builds the shared library, the static library, the Unix utility, the formatter, and the test systems. It tracks header dependencies and rebuilds objects when build options change.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">make           # builds/nifat32.so\nmake static    # builds/nifat32.a\nmake unix      # builds/unix_nifat32\nmake formatter # formatter/formatter\nmake tools     # formatter and Unix utility\nmake clean     # remove root build artifacts\nmake distclean # also clean the formatter\nmake help\n</code></pre>\n<p>The usual toolchain variables can be overridden:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">make CC=clang CFLAGS=&quot;-O3&quot; BUILD_DIR=out\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>CPPFLAGS</code>, <code>CFLAGS</code>, <code>LDFLAGS</code>, and <code>LDLIBS</code> may also be supplied or extended by the caller.</p>\n<h2 id=\"build-options\">Build options</h2>\n<p>Boolean Make variables accept <code>0</code> or <code>1</code> and default to <code>0</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Make variable</th>\n<th>C macro</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DEBUG</code></td>\n<td>-</td>\n<td>Adds <code>-O0 -g3</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>NIFAT32_RO</code></td>\n<td><code>NIFAT32_RO</code></td>\n<td>Compiles storage mutations as successful no-ops</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>NO_HEAP</code></td>\n<td><code>NO_HEAP</code></td>\n<td>Requests entry-cache, FAT-cache, FAT-map, and default-allocator disabling through header macros; pass the explicit flags for the current multi-file build</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER</code></td>\n<td><code>NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER</code></td>\n<td>Removes the built-in static allocator; callbacks must be supplied</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>NIFAT32_NO_ERROR</code></td>\n<td><code>NIFAT32_NO_ERROR</code></td>\n<td>Disables persistent error storage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE</code></td>\n<td><code>NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE</code></td>\n<td>Disables directory entry indexing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>NO_FAT_CACHE</code></td>\n<td><code>NO_FAT_CACHE</code></td>\n<td>Disables the in-memory FAT value cache</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>NO_FAT_MAP</code></td>\n<td><code>NO_FAT_MAP</code></td>\n<td>Disables the free-cluster bitmap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION</code></td>\n<td><code>NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION</code></td>\n<td>Skips directory-entry checksum validation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-</td>\n<td><code>NO_THREADSAFE</code></td>\n<td>Make the FS not thread safe (exclude related code from the final binary)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-</td>\n<td><code>NO_HAMMING</code></td>\n<td>Exclude all hamming-code related logic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-</td>\n<td><code>ENTRY_WITH_TIME</code></td>\n<td>Adds timestamp fields to directory entries; this changes the <code>create_entry</code> function signature and increases the size of each entry</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><code>ENTRY_WITH_TIME</code> is a C macro-only option. It stores FAT-style timestamp fields in each <code>directory_entry_t</code>, so fewer entries fit in one decoded directory cluster. Callers must also pass timestamp values to <code>create_entry</code>.</p>\n<p>For instance, this is the signature without this flag:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">create_entry(fullname, is_dir, first_cluster, file_size, entry);\n</code></pre>\n<p>And this is with <code>ENTRY_WITH_TIME</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">create_entry(\n    fullname,\n    is_dir,\n    first_cluster,\n    file_size,\n    second,\n    centisecond,\n    hour,\n    minute,\n    year,\n    mounth,\n    day,\n    entry\n);\n</code></pre>\n<p>Numeric configuration variables:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Make variable</th>\n<th>C macro</th>\n<th>Default</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ALLOC_BUFFER_SIZE</code></td>\n<td><code>ALLOC_BUFFER_SIZE</code></td>\n<td><code>512000</code></td>\n<td>Built-in allocator buffer size in bytes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE</code></td>\n<td><code>CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE</code></td>\n<td><code>50</code></td>\n<td>Maximum number of simultaneously open content entries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IO_THREADS_MAX</code></td>\n<td><code>IO_THREADS_MAX</code></td>\n<td><code>25</code></td>\n<td>Maximum number of tracked I/O lock areas</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><code>NO_HEAP=1</code> defines <code>NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE</code>, <code>NO_FAT_CACHE</code>, and <code>NO_FAT_MAP</code> inside <code>nifat32.h</code>. <code>NON_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER</code> is still accepted by the source as a legacy alias, but new builds should use <code>NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER</code>.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">make NIFAT32_RO=1 NO_HEAP=1\nmake ALLOC_BUFFER_SIZE=1048576 CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=100\nmake NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"logging-options\">Logging options</h2>\n<p>All log groups are disabled by default. Each Make variable enables the corresponding compile-time macro.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Make variable</th>\n<th>C macro</th>\n<th>Log category</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ERROR_LOGS</code></td>\n<td><code>ERROR_LOGS</code></td>\n<td>Errors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>WARN_LOGS</code></td>\n<td><code>WARNING_LOGS</code></td>\n<td>Warnings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>INFO_LOGS</code></td>\n<td><code>INFO_LOGS</code></td>\n<td>General information</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DEBUG_LOGS</code></td>\n<td><code>DEBUG_LOGS</code></td>\n<td>Debug messages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IO_LOGS</code></td>\n<td><code>IO_OPERATION_LOGS</code></td>\n<td>Disk operations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>MEM_LOGS</code></td>\n<td><code>MEM_OPERATION_LOGS</code></td>\n<td>Allocator operations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>LOGGING_LOGS</code></td>\n<td><code>LOGGING_LOGS</code></td>\n<td>Public API calls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>SPECIAL_LOGS</code></td>\n<td><code>SPECIAL_LOGS</code></td>\n<td>Special-purpose diagnostics</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">make ERROR_LOGS=1 WARN_LOGS=1 INFO_LOGS=1\nmake DEBUG=1 DEBUG_LOGS=1 IO_LOGS=1\n</code></pre>\n<p>Enabled logging also requires valid logging callbacks in <code>nifat32_params_t</code>. Passing <code>NULL</code> callbacks disables output at runtime.</p>","text":"Getting started The root Makefile builds the shared library, the static library, the Unix utility, the formatter, and the test systems. It tracks header dependencies and rebuilds objects when build options change. make # builds/nifat32.so make static # builds/nifat32.a make unix # builds/unix_nifat32 make formatter # formatter/formatter make tools # formatter and Unix utility make clean # remove root build artifacts make distclean # also clean the formatter make help The usual toolchain variables can be overridden: make CC=clang CFLAGS=\"-O3\" BUILD_DIR=out CPPFLAGS , CFLAGS , LDFLAGS , and LDLIBS may also be supplied or extended by the caller. Build options Boolean Make variables accept 0 or 1 and default to 0 . Make variable C macro Effect DEBUG - Adds -O0 -g3 NIFAT32_RO NIFAT32_RO Compiles storage mutations as successful no-ops NO_HEAP NO_HEAP Requests entry-cache, FAT-cache, FAT-map, and default-allocator disabling through header macros; pass the explicit flags for the current multi-file build NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER Removes the built-in static allocator; callbacks must be supplied NIFAT32_NO_ERROR NIFAT32_NO_ERROR Disables persistent error storage NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE Disables directory entry indexing NO_FAT_CACHE NO_FAT_CACHE Disables the in-memory FAT value cache NO_FAT_MAP NO_FAT_MAP Disables the free-cluster bitmap NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION Skips directory-entry checksum validation - NO_THREADSAFE Make the FS not thread safe (exclude related code from the final binary) - NO_HAMMING Exclude all hamming-code related logic - ENTRY_WITH_TIME Adds timestamp fields to directory entries; this changes the create_entry function signature and increases the size of each entry ENTRY_WITH_TIME is a C macro-only option. It stores FAT-style timestamp fields in each directory_entry_t , so fewer entries fit in one decoded directory cluster. Callers must also pass timestamp values to create_entry . For instance, this is the signature without this flag: create_entry(fullname, is_dir, first_cluster, file_size, entry); And this is with ENTRY_WITH_TIME : create_entry( fullname, is_dir, first_cluster, file_size, second, centisecond, hour, minute, year, mounth, day, entry ); Numeric configuration variables: Make variable C macro Default Effect ALLOC_BUFFER_SIZE ALLOC_BUFFER_SIZE 512000 Built-in allocator buffer size in bytes CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE 50 Maximum number of simultaneously open content entries IO_THREADS_MAX IO_THREADS_MAX 25 Maximum number of tracked I/O lock areas NO_HEAP=1 defines NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE , NO_FAT_CACHE , and NO_FAT_MAP inside nifat32.h . NON_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER is still accepted by the source as a legacy alias, but new builds should use NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER . Examples: make NIFAT32_RO=1 NO_HEAP=1 make ALLOC_BUFFER_SIZE=1048576 CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=100 make NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1 Logging options All log groups are disabled by default. Each Make variable enables the corresponding compile-time macro. Make variable C macro Log category ERROR_LOGS ERROR_LOGS Errors WARN_LOGS WARNING_LOGS Warnings INFO_LOGS INFO_LOGS General information DEBUG_LOGS DEBUG_LOGS Debug messages IO_LOGS IO_OPERATION_LOGS Disk operations MEM_LOGS MEM_OPERATION_LOGS Allocator operations LOGGING_LOGS LOGGING_LOGS Public API calls SPECIAL_LOGS SPECIAL_LOGS Special-purpose diagnostics make ERROR_LOGS=1 WARN_LOGS=1 INFO_LOGS=1 make DEBUG=1 DEBUG_LOGS=1 IO_LOGS=1 Enabled logging also requires valid logging callbacks in nifat32_params_t . Passing NULL callbacks disables output at runtime.","headings":[{"id":"build-options","title":"Build options","level":2},{"id":"logging-options","title":"Logging options","level":2}]},"docs/footprint":{"title":"Reference footprint","group":"Start here","source":"docs/footprint.md","html":"<h1 id=\"reference-footprint\">Reference footprint</h1>\n<p><strong>Minimum read-only:</strong> 6,767 B ROM · 856 B static RAM. Default RAM includes a 500 KiB allocator pool.</p>\n<div class=\"footprint-charts\"><section class=\"footprint-panel rom-panel\" aria-label=\"ROM · code + initialized data\">\n          <h3>ROM · code + initialized data</h3><div class=\"chart-controls\"></div><div class=\"chart-axis full-axis\"><span>0</span><span>25 KiB</span><span>50 KiB</span></div>\n\n          <div class=\"footprint-bars\"><div class=\"footprint-row pool-profile\">\n              <div class=\"bar-label\"><strong>Default · O2</strong><span>44.48 KiB <small>(45,544 B)</small></span></div>\n              <div class=\"bar-track\"><span style=\"--bar-full:88.9531%;--bar-zoom:100.0000%\"></span></div>\n              <p>Read/write · includes the 500 KiB allocator pool</p></div><div class=\"footprint-row\">\n              <div class=\"bar-label\"><strong>External allocator</strong><span>33.62 KiB <small>(34,425 B)</small></span></div>\n              <div class=\"bar-track\"><span style=\"--bar-full:67.2363%;--bar-zoom:100.0000%\"></span></div>\n              <p>Read-only · built-in allocator removed</p></div><div class=\"footprint-row\">\n              <div class=\"bar-label\"><strong>Caches disabled</strong><span>24.85 KiB <small>(25,449 B)</small></span></div>\n              <div class=\"bar-track\"><span style=\"--bar-full:49.7051%;--bar-zoom:100.0000%\"></span></div>\n              <p>Read-only · one entry, one I/O thread, no caches</p></div><div class=\"footprint-row\">\n              <div class=\"bar-label\"><strong>Minimum read-only</strong><span>6.61 KiB <small>(6,767 B)</small></span></div>\n              <div class=\"bar-track\"><span style=\"--bar-full:13.2168%;--bar-zoom:100.0000%\"></span></div>\n              <p>Read-only · Oz, LTO, public exports, reduced features</p></div></div></section><section class=\"footprint-panel ram-panel\" aria-label=\"Static RAM · data + bss\">\n          <h3>Static RAM · data + bss</h3><div class=\"chart-controls\"><label class=\"ram-zoom\"><input type=\"checkbox\"> Zoom RAM to 0–6 KiB <span>(omit default pool)</span></label></div><div class=\"chart-axis full-axis\"><span>0</span><span>280 KiB</span><span>560 KiB</span></div>\n          <div class=\"chart-axis zoom-axis\"><span>0</span><span>3 KiB</span><span>6 KiB</span></div>\n          <div class=\"footprint-bars\"><div class=\"footprint-row pool-profile\">\n              <div class=\"bar-label\"><strong>Default · O2</strong><span>505.23 KiB <small>(517,356 B)</small></span></div>\n              <div class=\"bar-track\"><span style=\"--bar-full:90.2197%;--bar-zoom:100.0000%\"></span></div>\n              <p>Read/write · includes the 500 KiB allocator pool</p></div><div class=\"footprint-row\">\n              <div class=\"bar-label\"><strong>External allocator</strong><span>5.07 KiB <small>(5,192 B)</small></span></div>\n              <div class=\"bar-track\"><span style=\"--bar-full:0.9054%;--bar-zoom:84.5052%\"></span></div>\n              <p>Read-only · built-in allocator removed</p></div><div class=\"footprint-row\">\n              <div class=\"bar-label\"><strong>Caches disabled</strong><span>1.27 KiB <small>(1,296 B)</small></span></div>\n              <div class=\"bar-track\"><span style=\"--bar-full:0.2260%;--bar-zoom:21.0938%\"></span></div>\n              <p>Read-only · one entry, one I/O thread, no caches</p></div><div class=\"footprint-row\">\n              <div class=\"bar-label\"><strong>Minimum read-only</strong><span>0.84 KiB <small>(856 B)</small></span></div>\n              <div class=\"bar-track\"><span style=\"--bar-full:0.1493%;--bar-zoom:13.9323%\"></span></div>\n              <p>Read-only · Oz, LTO, public exports, reduced features</p></div></div></section></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"measurement\">Measurement</h2>\n<p>Fedora x86-64 · GCC 15.2.1 · Binutils 2.45.1.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ROM:</strong> <code>text + data</code>. <strong>Static RAM:</strong> <code>data + bss</code>; excludes stacks and external allocation.</li>\n<li><strong>External allocator:</strong> <code>NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1</code> removes the built-in pool.</li>\n<li><strong>Minimum read-only:</strong> no mutations, caches, error reporting, entry validation or thread safety.</li>\n<li>Common flags: <code>-ffunction-sections -fdata-sections -Wl,--gc-sections</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>MCU sizes depend on the target toolchain.</p>\n<h2 id=\"all-16-measurements\">All 16 measurements</h2>\n<p>Complete flags per configuration; <code>-Os</code> and <code>-Oz</code> replace <code>-O2</code>.</p>\n<details>\n<summary>Show exact byte counts and build flags</summary>\n\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table class=\"footprint-table\"><thead><tr><th>Measured configuration</th><th>ROM</th><th>Static RAM</th><th>Active flags (complete set)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">01 · Default · O2</th><td>45,544 B</td><td>517,356 B</td><td><code>-O2</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">02 · Read-only</th><td>35,104 B</td><td>517,236 B</td><td><code>-O2</code><br><code>NIFAT32_RO=1</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">03 · No heap</th><td>35,104 B</td><td>517,236 B</td><td><code>-O2</code><br><code>NIFAT32_RO=1</code><br><code>NO_HEAP=1</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">04 · External allocator</th><td>34,425 B</td><td>5,192 B</td><td><code>-O2</code><br><code>NIFAT32_RO=1</code><br><code>NO_HEAP=1</code><br><code>NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">05 · No error reporting</th><td>32,869 B</td><td>5,172 B</td><td><code>-O2</code><br><code>NIFAT32_RO=1</code><br><code>NO_HEAP=1</code><br><code>NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">06 · No entry validation</th><td>32,845 B</td><td>5,172 B</td><td><code>-O2</code><br><code>NIFAT32_RO=1</code><br><code>NO_HEAP=1</code><br><code>NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1</code><br><code>NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">07 · 8 entries · 4 I/O threads</th><td>32,549 B</td><td>1,908 B</td><td><code>-O2</code><br><code>NIFAT32_RO=1</code><br><code>NO_HEAP=1</code><br><code>NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1</code><br><code>NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1</code><br><code>CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=8 IO_THREADS_MAX=4</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">08 · 1 entry · 1 I/O thread</th><td>31,957 B</td><td>1,348 B</td><td><code>-O2</code><br><code>NIFAT32_RO=1</code><br><code>NO_HEAP=1</code><br><code>NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1</code><br><code>NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1</code><br><code>CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 IO_THREADS_MAX=1</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">09 · Caches disabled</th><td>25,449 B</td><td>1,296 B</td><td><code>-O2</code><br><code>NIFAT32_RO=1</code><br><code>NO_HEAP=1</code><br><code>NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1</code><br><code>NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1</code><br><code>CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 IO_THREADS_MAX=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE=1 NO_FAT_CACHE=1 NO_FAT_MAP=1</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">10 · Optimize for size · Os</th><td>21,801 B</td><td>1,280 B</td><td><code>NIFAT32_RO=1</code><br><code>NO_HEAP=1</code><br><code>NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1</code><br><code>NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1</code><br><code>CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 IO_THREADS_MAX=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE=1 NO_FAT_CACHE=1 NO_FAT_MAP=1</code><br><code>-Os</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">11 · Optimize for size · Oz</th><td>21,490 B</td><td>1,280 B</td><td><code>NIFAT32_RO=1</code><br><code>NO_HEAP=1</code><br><code>NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1</code><br><code>NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1</code><br><code>CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 IO_THREADS_MAX=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE=1 NO_FAT_CACHE=1 NO_FAT_MAP=1</code><br><code>-Oz</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">12 · Link-time optimization</th><td>21,525 B</td><td>1,312 B</td><td><code>NIFAT32_RO=1</code><br><code>NO_HEAP=1</code><br><code>NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1</code><br><code>NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1</code><br><code>CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 IO_THREADS_MAX=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE=1 NO_FAT_CACHE=1 NO_FAT_MAP=1</code><br><code>-Oz</code><br><code>-flto</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">13 · No thread safety</th><td>21,257 B</td><td>1,312 B</td><td><code>NIFAT32_RO=1</code><br><code>NO_HEAP=1</code><br><code>NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1</code><br><code>NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1</code><br><code>CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 IO_THREADS_MAX=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE=1 NO_FAT_CACHE=1 NO_FAT_MAP=1</code><br><code>-Oz</code><br><code>-flto</code><br><code>NO_THREADSAFE=1</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">14 · No unwind tables</th><td>17,001 B</td><td>1,312 B</td><td><code>NIFAT32_RO=1</code><br><code>NO_HEAP=1</code><br><code>NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1</code><br><code>NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1</code><br><code>CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 IO_THREADS_MAX=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE=1 NO_FAT_CACHE=1 NO_FAT_MAP=1</code><br><code>-Oz</code><br><code>-flto</code><br><code>NO_THREADSAFE=1</code><br><code>-fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables -fno-unwind-tables</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">15 · Export public API only</th><td>6,782 B</td><td>856 B</td><td><code>NIFAT32_RO=1</code><br><code>NO_HEAP=1</code><br><code>NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1</code><br><code>NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1</code><br><code>CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 IO_THREADS_MAX=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE=1 NO_FAT_CACHE=1 NO_FAT_MAP=1</code><br><code>-Oz</code><br><code>-flto</code><br><code>NO_THREADSAFE=1</code><br><code>-fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables -fno-unwind-tables</code><br><code>-Wl,--version-script=nifat32.exports</code></td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">16 · Minimum read-only</th><td>6,767 B</td><td>856 B</td><td><code>NIFAT32_RO=1</code><br><code>NO_HEAP=1</code><br><code>NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1</code><br><code>NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1</code><br><code>CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 IO_THREADS_MAX=1</code><br><code>NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE=1 NO_FAT_CACHE=1 NO_FAT_MAP=1</code><br><code>-Oz</code><br><code>-flto</code><br><code>NO_THREADSAFE=1</code><br><code>-fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables -fno-unwind-tables</code><br><code>-Wl,--version-script=nifat32.exports</code><br><code>-fno-plt -Wl,--build-id=none -Wl,-z,noseparate-code</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"maximum-profile\">Maximum profile</h2>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Profile</th>\n<th>Configuration</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">ROM</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Static RAM</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">ELF file</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Stripped ELF</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum feature/debug profile</td>\n<td><code>-O0 -g3</code>, all <code>*_LOGS=1</code>, default features and numeric limits</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>63.9 KiB</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>505.2 KiB</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">164.6 KiB</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">71.1 KiB</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Minimum measured build:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">make shared \\\n  BUILD_DIR=builds/minimum \\\n  CFLAGS=&quot;-Oz -flto -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections -fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables -fno-unwind-tables -fno-plt&quot; \\\n  LDFLAGS=&quot;-flto -Wl,--gc-sections -Wl,--version-script=nifat32.exports -Wl,--build-id=none -Wl,-z,noseparate-code&quot; \\\n  NIFAT32_RO=1 \\\n  NO_HEAP=1 \\\n  NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1 \\\n  NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE=1 \\\n  NO_FAT_CACHE=1 \\\n  NO_FAT_MAP=1 \\\n  NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1 \\\n  NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1 \\\n  NO_THREADSAFE=1 \\\n  CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 \\\n  IO_THREADS_MAX=1\n</code></pre>\n<p>Maximum feature/debug build:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">make shared \\\n  BUILD_DIR=builds/maximum \\\n  CFLAGS=&quot;-O0 -g3&quot; \\\n  ERROR_LOGS=1 WARN_LOGS=1 INFO_LOGS=1 DEBUG_LOGS=1 \\\n  IO_LOGS=1 MEM_LOGS=1 LOGGING_LOGS=1 SPECIAL_LOGS=1\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"on-a-chip\">On a chip</h2>\n<p><a href=\"#/builder\">Filesystem builder</a>: STM32, ESP32 and ATmega flash budgets and copy placement. The spatial map models placement; it does not represent measured silicon coordinates.</p>","text":"Reference footprint Minimum read-only: 6,767 B ROM · 856 B static RAM. Default RAM includes a 500 KiB allocator pool. ROM · code + initialized data 0 25 KiB 50 KiB Default · O2 44.48 KiB (45,544 B) Read/write · includes the 500 KiB allocator pool External allocator 33.62 KiB (34,425 B) Read-only · built-in allocator removed Caches disabled 24.85 KiB (25,449 B) Read-only · one entry, one I/O thread, no caches Minimum read-only 6.61 KiB (6,767 B) Read-only · Oz, LTO, public exports, reduced features Static RAM · data + bss Zoom RAM to 0–6 KiB (omit default pool) 0 280 KiB 560 KiB 0 3 KiB 6 KiB Default · O2 505.23 KiB (517,356 B) Read/write · includes the 500 KiB allocator pool External allocator 5.07 KiB (5,192 B) Read-only · built-in allocator removed Caches disabled 1.27 KiB (1,296 B) Read-only · one entry, one I/O thread, no caches Minimum read-only 0.84 KiB (856 B) Read-only · Oz, LTO, public exports, reduced features Measurement Fedora x86-64 · GCC 15.2.1 · Binutils 2.45.1. ROM: text + data . Static RAM: data + bss ; excludes stacks and external allocation. External allocator: NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1 removes the built-in pool. Minimum read-only: no mutations, caches, error reporting, entry validation or thread safety. Common flags: -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections -Wl,--gc-sections . MCU sizes depend on the target toolchain. All 16 measurements Complete flags per configuration; -Os and -Oz replace -O2 . Show exact byte counts and build flags Measured configuration ROM Static RAM Active flags (complete set) 01 · Default · O2 45,544 B 517,356 B -O2 02 · Read-only 35,104 B 517,236 B -O2 NIFAT32_RO=1 03 · No heap 35,104 B 517,236 B -O2 NIFAT32_RO=1 NO_HEAP=1 04 · External allocator 34,425 B 5,192 B -O2 NIFAT32_RO=1 NO_HEAP=1 NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1 05 · No error reporting 32,869 B 5,172 B -O2 NIFAT32_RO=1 NO_HEAP=1 NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1 NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1 06 · No entry validation 32,845 B 5,172 B -O2 NIFAT32_RO=1 NO_HEAP=1 NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1 NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1 NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1 07 · 8 entries · 4 I/O threads 32,549 B 1,908 B -O2 NIFAT32_RO=1 NO_HEAP=1 NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1 NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1 NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1 CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=8 IO_THREADS_MAX=4 08 · 1 entry · 1 I/O thread 31,957 B 1,348 B -O2 NIFAT32_RO=1 NO_HEAP=1 NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1 NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1 NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1 CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 IO_THREADS_MAX=1 09 · Caches disabled 25,449 B 1,296 B -O2 NIFAT32_RO=1 NO_HEAP=1 NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1 NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1 NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1 CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 IO_THREADS_MAX=1 NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE=1 NO_FAT_CACHE=1 NO_FAT_MAP=1 10 · Optimize for size · Os 21,801 B 1,280 B NIFAT32_RO=1 NO_HEAP=1 NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1 NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1 NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1 CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 IO_THREADS_MAX=1 NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE=1 NO_FAT_CACHE=1 NO_FAT_MAP=1 -Os 11 · Optimize for size · Oz 21,490 B 1,280 B NIFAT32_RO=1 NO_HEAP=1 NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1 NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1 NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1 CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 IO_THREADS_MAX=1 NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE=1 NO_FAT_CACHE=1 NO_FAT_MAP=1 -Oz 12 · Link-time optimization 21,525 B 1,312 B NIFAT32_RO=1 NO_HEAP=1 NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1 NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1 NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1 CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 IO_THREADS_MAX=1 NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE=1 NO_FAT_CACHE=1 NO_FAT_MAP=1 -Oz -flto 13 · No thread safety 21,257 B 1,312 B NIFAT32_RO=1 NO_HEAP=1 NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1 NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1 NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1 CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 IO_THREADS_MAX=1 NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE=1 NO_FAT_CACHE=1 NO_FAT_MAP=1 -Oz -flto NO_THREADSAFE=1 14 · No unwind tables 17,001 B 1,312 B NIFAT32_RO=1 NO_HEAP=1 NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1 NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1 NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1 CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 IO_THREADS_MAX=1 NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE=1 NO_FAT_CACHE=1 NO_FAT_MAP=1 -Oz -flto NO_THREADSAFE=1 -fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables -fno-unwind-tables 15 · Export public API only 6,782 B 856 B NIFAT32_RO=1 NO_HEAP=1 NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1 NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1 NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1 CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 IO_THREADS_MAX=1 NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE=1 NO_FAT_CACHE=1 NO_FAT_MAP=1 -Oz -flto NO_THREADSAFE=1 -fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables -fno-unwind-tables -Wl,--version-script=nifat32.exports 16 · Minimum read-only 6,767 B 856 B NIFAT32_RO=1 NO_HEAP=1 NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1 NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1 NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1 CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 IO_THREADS_MAX=1 NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE=1 NO_FAT_CACHE=1 NO_FAT_MAP=1 -Oz -flto NO_THREADSAFE=1 -fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables -fno-unwind-tables -Wl,--version-script=nifat32.exports -fno-plt -Wl,--build-id=none -Wl,-z,noseparate-code Maximum profile Profile Configuration ROM Static RAM ELF file Stripped ELF Maximum feature/debug profile -O0 -g3 , all *_LOGS=1 , default features and numeric limits 63.9 KiB 505.2 KiB 164.6 KiB 71.1 KiB Minimum measured build: make shared \\ BUILD_DIR=builds/minimum \\ CFLAGS=\"-Oz -flto -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections -fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables -fno-unwind-tables -fno-plt\" \\ LDFLAGS=\"-flto -Wl,--gc-sections -Wl,--version-script=nifat32.exports -Wl,--build-id=none -Wl,-z,noseparate-code\" \\ NIFAT32_RO=1 \\ NO_HEAP=1 \\ NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1 \\ NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE=1 \\ NO_FAT_CACHE=1 \\ NO_FAT_MAP=1 \\ NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1 \\ NO_ENTRY_VALIDATION=1 \\ NO_THREADSAFE=1 \\ CONTENT_TABLE_SIZE=1 \\ IO_THREADS_MAX=1 Maximum feature/debug build: make shared \\ BUILD_DIR=builds/maximum \\ CFLAGS=\"-O0 -g3\" \\ ERROR_LOGS=1 WARN_LOGS=1 INFO_LOGS=1 DEBUG_LOGS=1 \\ IO_LOGS=1 MEM_LOGS=1 LOGGING_LOGS=1 SPECIAL_LOGS=1 On a chip Filesystem builder : STM32, ESP32 and ATmega flash budgets and copy placement. The spatial map models placement; it does not represent measured silicon coordinates.","headings":[{"id":"measurement","title":"Measurement","level":2},{"id":"all-16-measurements","title":"All 16 measurements","level":2},{"id":"maximum-profile","title":"Maximum profile","level":2},{"id":"on-a-chip","title":"On a chip","level":2}]},"docs/architecture":{"title":"Architecture","group":"Understand","source":"docs/architecture.md","html":"<h1 id=\"architecture\">Architecture</h1>\n<p>NiFAT32 looks like FAT32 from the API side but is not wire-compatible. Every metadata structure is stored in redundant copies, spread across the whole volume, encoded, and checksummed, so a single-event upset (SEU) or a local multiple-event upset (MEU/scratch) corrupts at most one or a few copies instead of the structure.</p>\n<h2 id=\"on-disk-layout\">On-disk layout</h2>\n<p>The image is a sequence of 512-byte sectors. Apart from a small fixed prefix, NiFAT32 does not place its structures in fixed blocks: the boot sector copies, FAT copies, journal areas, and error-storage areas are each placed at a pseudo-random sector chosen by a deterministic 32-bit mixer, so different copies of the same structure land far apart on the media.</p>\n<pre><code>| gap |  cluster gap (no data clusters)              | data clusters, root dir is cluster 2 ... |\n| 0-1 |  32 .. 32 + fc*fatsize                        |  32 + fc*fatsize .. ts-1                     |\n</code></pre>\n<p>On top of this map the formatter scatters:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Structure</th>\n<th>Copies</th>\n<th>Placement</th>\n<th>On-disk size</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Boot sector</td>\n<td><code>bsbc</code></td>\n<td>sector <code>2 + mix32(i + BOOT_SEED) % (ts - 2)</code></td>\n<td>1 sector (encoded structure, zero-padded)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FAT</td>\n<td><code>fc</code></td>\n<td>sector <code>55 + mix32(i + FAT_SEED) % (ts - 23)</code></td>\n<td><code>2 * fat_size</code> sectors (Hamming-encoded table)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Journal</td>\n<td><code>jc</code></td>\n<td>sector <code>2 + mix32(i + JOURNAL_SEED) % (ts - 2)</code></td>\n<td><code>spc</code> sectors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Error storage</td>\n<td><code>ec</code></td>\n<td>sector <code>2 + mix32(i + ERRORS_SEED) % (ts - 2)</code></td>\n<td><code>spc</code> sectors</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><code>mix32</code> is a small deterministic mixer (shift-multiply-shift), each structure type has its own seed, and the placement depends only on the copy index and the total sector count. The same formulas run in the formatter and in the library, so the mount never searches for structures — it computes where they are.</p>\n<p>Cluster numbering keeps the FAT32 convention: cluster 0 and 1 are reserved, the root directory lives at cluster 2, and cluster <code>n</code> occupies sectors <code>(n - 2) * spc + first_data_sector</code>, where <code>first_data_sector = reserved_sector_count + fc * fat_size</code> (<code>reserved_sector_count</code> is 32 in the reference geometry). The FAT table itself covers all <code>total_clusters</code> entries, and its size is solved iteratively so the table fits its own allocated space.</p>\n<p>File cluster chains are also started at pseudo-random offsets within the free space, so a scratch that destroys one neighborhood does not take out all clusters of the hottest files.</p>\n<h2 id=\"protection-layers\">Protection layers</h2>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>What it protects</th>\n<th>How</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Hamming(15,11)</td>\n<td>Every stored byte of boot sectors, FAT, journals, directory entries, error records</td>\n<td>Each byte is expanded to a 15-bit word that corrects a single flipped bit; decoding happens before any checksum</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Murmur3 checksum</td>\n<td>Boot sector (base + extended section), directory entries, journal entries</td>\n<td>Recomputed on read; a mismatch means the copy is untrusted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Redundant copies</td>\n<td>Boot sectors (<code>bsbc</code>), FAT (<code>fc</code>), journals (<code>jc</code>), error records (<code>ec</code>)</td>\n<td>Copies are spread by the mixer; damaged copies are replaced from good ones</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Majority voting</td>\n<td>Every FAT entry read, every journal entry read</td>\n<td>The value from the majority of copies wins; on disagreement the winner is written back to all copies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Journaling (WAL)</td>\n<td>Directory entry add/edit/delete</td>\n<td>The operation is recorded in all journal copies before the directory cluster is modified; on mount, unsolved entries are replayed and then cleared</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Error ring</td>\n<td>Post-mortem diagnostics</td>\n<td>Recovered faults are registered as error codes that the host can consume with <code>NIFAT32_get_last_error()</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The layers are ordered so a radiation event meets several independent obstacles: a bit flipped inside one copy is corrected by the Hamming word; if it is not, the copy checksum fails and the copy loses the vote; the vote is repaired from the other copies; a scratch wide enough to hit several copies still has to hit a strict majority of <em>all</em> copies of <em>every</em> structure, which the pseudo-random placement makes unlikely.</p>\n<h2 id=\"mount-sequence\">Mount sequence</h2>\n<pre><code>1. Read boot sector copy #bs_num at its computed sector\n2. Decode (Hamming), verify both Murmur3 checksums\n3. Checksum mismatch  -&gt;  register CHECKSUM_CHECK_ERROR, bs_num++, retry (up to bs_count)\n4. If bs_num &gt; 0      -&gt;  rewrite the good encoded boot sector to all other copies\n5. Compute geometry from the boot sector (spc, fat_size, fat_count, total sectors)\n6. Optionally load the FAT cache (lazy, full load, free-cluster bitmap)\n7. Replay unsolved journal entries from all journal copies\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"write-sequence-file\">Write sequence (file)</h2>\n<pre><code>1. Lookup the path (name hashes + optional entry cache)\n2. Allocate clusters (pseudo-random start offset within free space)\n3. write_fat: update the FAT entry in ALL fc copies (plus the cache and free-cluster bitmap)\n4. Journal: record the directory entry operation in all jc journal copies\n5. Write the encoded directory entry (with checksum) into the directory cluster\n6. Journal: clear the slot (NO_OP) in all copies\n7. Write the file data into the allocated clusters\n</code></pre>\n<p>The journal slot is written before the directory cluster, so a power loss between the two steps leaves an unsolved entry that the next mount replays. The journal entry carries the full squeezed directory entry, not just the operation, which is why a replay is a plain rewrite.</p>\n<h2 id=\"read-sequence-fat-entry\">Read sequence (FAT entry)</h2>\n<pre><code>1. Cache hit and value is not BAD -&gt; return the cached value\n2. Otherwise read the entry from all fc FAT copies\n3. Hamming-decode each copy\n4. Majority vote over the decoded values\n5. Any disagreement -&gt; write the winning value back to all fc copies\n6. Cache the result\n</code></pre>\n<p>The same voting routine is used for journal entries, compared by checksum.</p>\n<h2 id=\"persistent-error-ring\">Persistent error ring</h2>\n<p>With <code>ec &gt; 0</code> the library keeps <code>ec</code> pseudo-randomly placed areas of <code>spc</code> sectors and registers error codes there as they happen. The host pulls them out one at a time:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">error_code_t error = NIFAT32_get_last_error(); // -1 when the ring is empty\n</code></pre>\n<p>The ring is the only place where a fault is recorded <em>before</em> it is repaired, so the host can count and classify upsets even when the filesystem keeps working.</p>","text":"Architecture NiFAT32 looks like FAT32 from the API side but is not wire-compatible. Every metadata structure is stored in redundant copies, spread across the whole volume, encoded, and checksummed, so a single-event upset (SEU) or a local multiple-event upset (MEU/scratch) corrupts at most one or a few copies instead of the structure. On-disk layout The image is a sequence of 512-byte sectors. Apart from a small fixed prefix, NiFAT32 does not place its structures in fixed blocks: the boot sector copies, FAT copies, journal areas, and error-storage areas are each placed at a pseudo-random sector chosen by a deterministic 32-bit mixer, so different copies of the same structure land far apart on the media. | gap | cluster gap (no data clusters) | data clusters, root dir is cluster 2 ... | | 0-1 | 32 .. 32 + fc*fatsize | 32 + fc*fatsize .. ts-1 | On top of this map the formatter scatters: Structure Copies Placement On-disk size Boot sector bsbc sector 2 + mix32(i + BOOT_SEED) % (ts - 2) 1 sector (encoded structure, zero-padded) FAT fc sector 55 + mix32(i + FAT_SEED) % (ts - 23) 2 * fat_size sectors (Hamming-encoded table) Journal jc sector 2 + mix32(i + JOURNAL_SEED) % (ts - 2) spc sectors Error storage ec sector 2 + mix32(i + ERRORS_SEED) % (ts - 2) spc sectors mix32 is a small deterministic mixer (shift-multiply-shift), each structure type has its own seed, and the placement depends only on the copy index and the total sector count. The same formulas run in the formatter and in the library, so the mount never searches for structures — it computes where they are. Cluster numbering keeps the FAT32 convention: cluster 0 and 1 are reserved, the root directory lives at cluster 2, and cluster n occupies sectors (n - 2) * spc + first_data_sector , where first_data_sector = reserved_sector_count + fc * fat_size ( reserved_sector_count is 32 in the reference geometry). The FAT table itself covers all total_clusters entries, and its size is solved iteratively so the table fits its own allocated space. File cluster chains are also started at pseudo-random offsets within the free space, so a scratch that destroys one neighborhood does not take out all clusters of the hottest files. Protection layers Layer What it protects How Hamming(15,11) Every stored byte of boot sectors, FAT, journals, directory entries, error records Each byte is expanded to a 15-bit word that corrects a single flipped bit; decoding happens before any checksum Murmur3 checksum Boot sector (base + extended section), directory entries, journal entries Recomputed on read; a mismatch means the copy is untrusted Redundant copies Boot sectors ( bsbc ), FAT ( fc ), journals ( jc ), error records ( ec ) Copies are spread by the mixer; damaged copies are replaced from good ones Majority voting Every FAT entry read, every journal entry read The value from the majority of copies wins; on disagreement the winner is written back to all copies Journaling (WAL) Directory entry add/edit/delete The operation is recorded in all journal copies before the directory cluster is modified; on mount, unsolved entries are replayed and then cleared Error ring Post-mortem diagnostics Recovered faults are registered as error codes that the host can consume with NIFAT32_get_last_error() The layers are ordered so a radiation event meets several independent obstacles: a bit flipped inside one copy is corrected by the Hamming word; if it is not, the copy checksum fails and the copy loses the vote; the vote is repaired from the other copies; a scratch wide enough to hit several copies still has to hit a strict majority of all copies of every structure, which the pseudo-random placement makes unlikely. Mount sequence 1. Read boot sector copy #bs_num at its computed sector 2. Decode (Hamming), verify both Murmur3 checksums 3. Checksum mismatch -> register CHECKSUM_CHECK_ERROR, bs_num++, retry (up to bs_count) 4. If bs_num > 0 -> rewrite the good encoded boot sector to all other copies 5. Compute geometry from the boot sector (spc, fat_size, fat_count, total sectors) 6. Optionally load the FAT cache (lazy, full load, free-cluster bitmap) 7. Replay unsolved journal entries from all journal copies Write sequence (file) 1. Lookup the path (name hashes + optional entry cache) 2. Allocate clusters (pseudo-random start offset within free space) 3. write_fat: update the FAT entry in ALL fc copies (plus the cache and free-cluster bitmap) 4. Journal: record the directory entry operation in all jc journal copies 5. Write the encoded directory entry (with checksum) into the directory cluster 6. Journal: clear the slot (NO_OP) in all copies 7. Write the file data into the allocated clusters The journal slot is written before the directory cluster, so a power loss between the two steps leaves an unsolved entry that the next mount replays. The journal entry carries the full squeezed directory entry, not just the operation, which is why a replay is a plain rewrite. Read sequence (FAT entry) 1. Cache hit and value is not BAD -> return the cached value 2. Otherwise read the entry from all fc FAT copies 3. Hamming-decode each copy 4. Majority vote over the decoded values 5. Any disagreement -> write the winning value back to all fc copies 6. Cache the result The same voting routine is used for journal entries, compared by checksum. Persistent error ring With ec > 0 the library keeps ec pseudo-randomly placed areas of spc sectors and registers error codes there as they happen. The host pulls them out one at a time: error_code_t error = NIFAT32_get_last_error(); // -1 when the ring is empty The ring is the only place where a fault is recorded before it is repaired, so the host can count and classify upsets even when the filesystem keeps working.","headings":[{"id":"on-disk-layout","title":"On-disk layout","level":2},{"id":"protection-layers","title":"Protection layers","level":2},{"id":"mount-sequence","title":"Mount sequence","level":2},{"id":"write-sequence-file","title":"Write sequence (file)","level":2},{"id":"read-sequence-fat-entry","title":"Read sequence (FAT entry)","level":2},{"id":"persistent-error-ring","title":"Persistent error ring","level":2}]},"docs/benchmarks":{"title":"Benchmarks & comparisons","group":"Understand","source":"docs/benchmarks.md","html":"<h1 id=\"benchmarks-comparisons\">Benchmarks &amp; comparisons</h1>\n<p><strong>NiFAT32 configuration:</strong> five boot copies, five FAT copies, five metadata backups; caches and indexing disabled.</p>\n<h2 id=\"throughput\">Throughput</h2>\n<p>NiFAT32 throughput divided by the comparison filesystem's throughput. <strong>Above 1× is faster.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"comparison-chart\"><div class=\"comparison-chart-top\"><div><p class=\"eyebrow\">RELATIVE THROUGHPUT</p>\n      <h3>Throughput by operation</h3></div><label>Compare with<select data-throughput-comparison><option>LittleFS</option><option>FAT32</option><option>YAFFS2</option></select></label></div><div class=\"comparison-panel\" data-comparison=\"LittleFS\" style=\"--baseline:8.33333%\">\n          <p class=\"comparison-baseline\">LittleFS = 1× <span>· higher is faster</span></p>\n          <div class=\"throughput-axis\"><span>0</span><span>6×</span><span>12×</span></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-rows\"><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Open</span><strong>3.48×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:29.02589%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Create</span><strong>5.52×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:46.04052%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Write</span><strong>5.96×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:49.69191%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Read</span><strong>3.26×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:27.14441%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Rename</span><strong>10.4×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:86.87795%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Copy</span><strong>4.87×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:40.55150%\"></span></div></div></div></div><div class=\"comparison-panel\" data-comparison=\"FAT32\" hidden style=\"--baseline:100.00000%\">\n          <p class=\"comparison-baseline\">FAT32 = 1× <span>· higher is faster</span></p>\n          <div class=\"throughput-axis\"><span>0</span><span>0.5×</span><span>1×</span></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-rows\"><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Open</span><strong>0.0617×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:6.17284%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Create</span><strong>0.231×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:23.10536%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Write</span><strong>0.14×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:13.98210%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Read</span><strong>0.132×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:13.23276%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Rename</span><strong>0.286×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:28.55511%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Copy</span><strong>0.247×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:24.65483%\"></span></div></div></div></div><div class=\"comparison-panel\" data-comparison=\"YAFFS2\" hidden style=\"--baseline:40.00000%\">\n          <p class=\"comparison-baseline\">YAFFS2 = 1× <span>· higher is faster</span></p>\n          <div class=\"throughput-axis\"><span>0</span><span>1.25×</span><span>2.5×</span></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-rows\"><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Open</span><strong>0.00829×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:0.33140%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Create</span><strong>0.83×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:33.19502%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Write</span><strong>2.16×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:86.59883%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Read</span><strong>0.28×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:11.19507%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Rename</span><strong>0.747×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:29.87304%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Copy</span><strong>0.564×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:22.56063%\"></span></div></div></div></div></div>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Exact throughput ratios</summary>\n\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>vs FAT32</th><th>vs LittleFS</th><th>vs YAFFS2</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Open</th><td>0.06173×</td><td>3.483×</td><td>0.008285×</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Create</th><td>0.2311×</td><td>5.525×</td><td>0.8299×</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Write</th><td>0.1398×</td><td>5.963×</td><td>2.165×</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Read</th><td>0.1323×</td><td>3.257×</td><td>0.2799×</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Rename</th><td>0.2856×</td><td>10.43×</td><td>0.7468×</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Copy</th><td>0.2465×</td><td>4.866×</td><td>0.564×</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"energy-per-operation\">Energy per operation</h2>\n<p>Chart: NiFAT32 energy / comparison filesystem energy. <strong>Below 1× uses less energy.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"comparison-chart\"><div class=\"comparison-chart-top\"><div><p class=\"eyebrow\">GROSS ENERGY / OPERATION</p>\n      <h3>Energy per operation</h3></div><label>Compare with<select data-throughput-comparison><option>LittleFS</option><option>FAT32</option><option>YAFFS2</option></select></label></div><div class=\"comparison-panel\" data-comparison=\"LittleFS\" style=\"--baseline:100.00000%\">\n          <p class=\"comparison-baseline\">LittleFS = 1× <span>· lower uses less energy</span></p>\n          <div class=\"throughput-axis\"><span>0</span><span>0.5×</span><span>1×</span></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-rows\"><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Open</span><strong>0.234×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:23.36449%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Create</span><strong>0.163×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:16.26016%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Write</span><strong>0.135×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:13.52448%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Read</span><strong>0.25×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:24.95633%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Rename</span><strong>0.0862×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:8.62069%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Copy</span><strong>0.181×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:18.10610%\"></span></div></div></div></div><div class=\"comparison-panel\" data-comparison=\"FAT32\" hidden style=\"--baseline:8.33333%\">\n          <p class=\"comparison-baseline\">FAT32 = 1× <span>· lower uses less energy</span></p>\n          <div class=\"throughput-axis\"><span>0</span><span>6×</span><span>12×</span></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-rows\"><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Open</span><strong>9.88×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:82.34519%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Create</span><strong>3.76×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:31.31655%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Write</span><strong>5.98×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:49.87034%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Read</span><strong>6.53×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:54.43066%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Rename</span><strong>2.79×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:23.21263%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Copy</span><strong>3.66×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:30.49152%\"></span></div></div></div></div><div class=\"comparison-panel\" data-comparison=\"YAFFS2\" hidden style=\"--baseline:0.80000%\">\n          <p class=\"comparison-baseline\">YAFFS2 = 1× <span>· lower uses less energy</span></p>\n          <div class=\"throughput-axis\"><span>0</span><span>62.5×</span><span>125×</span></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-rows\"><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Open</span><strong>122×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:97.51341%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Create</span><strong>1.23×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:0.98244%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Write</span><strong>0.479×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:0.38333%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Read</span><strong>3.65×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:2.91971%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Rename</span><strong>1.41×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:1.12581%\"></span></div></div><div class=\"throughput-row\"><div class=\"throughput-label\"><span>Copy</span><strong>1.86×</strong></div>\n          <div class=\"throughput-track\"><span style=\"width:1.49031%\"></span></div></div></div></div></div>\n\n<details>\n<summary>Exact energy ratios: comparison filesystem / NiFAT32</summary>\n\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>vs FAT32</th><th>vs LittleFS</th><th>vs YAFFS2</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Open</th><td>0.1012×</td><td>4.28×</td><td>0.008204×</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Create</th><td>0.2661×</td><td>6.15×</td><td>0.8143×</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Write</th><td>0.1671×</td><td>7.394×</td><td>2.087×</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Read</th><td>0.1531×</td><td>4.007×</td><td>0.274×</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Rename</th><td>0.359×</td><td>11.6×</td><td>0.7106×</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Copy</th><td>0.2733×</td><td>5.523×</td><td>0.5368×</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"fault-tolerance\">Fault tolerance</h2>\n<div class=\"research-chart\"><div class=\"research-chart-top\"><div><span class=\"eyebrow\">BIT-FLIP INJECTION</span><h3>Failed runs</h3></div><label>Image size <select data-fault-volume><option value=\"100\">100 MB</option><option value=\"200\">200 MB</option><option value=\"300\">300 MB</option><option value=\"400\">400 MB</option><option value=\"500\">500 MB</option></select></label></div><figure data-volume=\"100\">\n          <div class=\"research-plot-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable fault injection chart\">\n            <img src=\"assets/charts/faults-100.svg\" width=\"800\" height=\"420\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Failed runs versus one to ten million injected bit flips, 100 MB image. At ten million flips: FAT32: 99, NiFAT32: 14, LittleFS + RS: 13. Lower counts are better.\"></div>\n          <figcaption><span>At 10 million injected flips</span><div class=\"fault-counts\"><div style=\"--series:#b7a799\"><strong>99</strong><span>FAT32</span></div><div style=\"--series:#ed6942\"><strong>14</strong><span>NiFAT32</span></div><div style=\"--series:#a4c69c\"><strong>13</strong><span>LittleFS + RS</span></div></div></figcaption></figure><figure data-volume=\"200\" hidden>\n          <div class=\"research-plot-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable fault injection chart\">\n            <img src=\"assets/charts/faults-200.svg\" width=\"800\" height=\"420\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Failed runs versus one to ten million injected bit flips, 200 MB image. At ten million flips: FAT32: 85, NiFAT32: 0, LittleFS + RS: 0. Lower counts are better.\"></div>\n          <figcaption><span>At 10 million injected flips</span><div class=\"fault-counts\"><div style=\"--series:#b7a799\"><strong>85</strong><span>FAT32</span></div><div style=\"--series:#ed6942\"><strong>0</strong><span>NiFAT32</span></div><div style=\"--series:#a4c69c\"><strong>0</strong><span>LittleFS + RS</span></div></div></figcaption></figure><figure data-volume=\"300\" hidden>\n          <div class=\"research-plot-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable fault injection chart\">\n            <img src=\"assets/charts/faults-300.svg\" width=\"800\" height=\"420\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Failed runs versus one to ten million injected bit flips, 300 MB image. At ten million flips: FAT32: 76, NiFAT32: 0, LittleFS + RS: 0. Lower counts are better.\"></div>\n          <figcaption><span>At 10 million injected flips</span><div class=\"fault-counts\"><div style=\"--series:#b7a799\"><strong>76</strong><span>FAT32</span></div><div style=\"--series:#ed6942\"><strong>0</strong><span>NiFAT32</span></div><div style=\"--series:#a4c69c\"><strong>0</strong><span>LittleFS + RS</span></div></div></figcaption></figure><figure data-volume=\"400\" hidden>\n          <div class=\"research-plot-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable fault injection chart\">\n            <img src=\"assets/charts/faults-400.svg\" width=\"800\" height=\"420\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Failed runs versus one to ten million injected bit flips, 400 MB image. At ten million flips: FAT32: 49, NiFAT32: 0, LittleFS + RS: 0. Lower counts are better.\"></div>\n          <figcaption><span>At 10 million injected flips</span><div class=\"fault-counts\"><div style=\"--series:#b7a799\"><strong>49</strong><span>FAT32</span></div><div style=\"--series:#ed6942\"><strong>0</strong><span>NiFAT32</span></div><div style=\"--series:#a4c69c\"><strong>0</strong><span>LittleFS + RS</span></div></div></figcaption></figure><figure data-volume=\"500\" hidden>\n          <div class=\"research-plot-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable fault injection chart\">\n            <img src=\"assets/charts/faults-500.svg\" width=\"800\" height=\"420\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"Failed runs versus one to ten million injected bit flips, 500 MB image. At ten million flips: FAT32: 55, NiFAT32: 0, LittleFS + RS: 0. Lower counts are better.\"></div>\n          <figcaption><span>At 10 million injected flips</span><div class=\"fault-counts\"><div style=\"--series:#b7a799\"><strong>55</strong><span>FAT32</span></div><div style=\"--series:#ed6942\"><strong>0</strong><span>NiFAT32</span></div><div style=\"--series:#a4c69c\"><strong>0</strong><span>LittleFS + RS</span></div></div></figcaption></figure><p class=\"chart-note\">Failed mount or file access. Payload integrity excluded. Lower is better.</p></div>\n\n<p>LittleFS uses the <code>ramrsbd</code> Reed–Solomon block layer in the fault-injection test.</p>\n<h2 id=\"advantages-tradeoffs\">Advantages &amp; tradeoffs</h2>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Compared with</th>\n<th>NiFAT32 advantage</th>\n<th>Tradeoff</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>FAT32</td>\n<td>Fewer failed runs under bit-flip injection; metadata correction and repair</td>\n<td>FAT32 is 3.50–16.2× faster in the measured operations and uses less energy.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>LittleFS</td>\n<td>3.26–10.43× the measured throughput; lower gross energy per operation</td>\n<td>LittleFS + RS has slightly fewer failures in the fault-injection test.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>YAFFS2</td>\n<td>2.16× write throughput; about 48% of the gross write energy</td>\n<td>YAFFS2 is faster and uses less energy for the other five operations.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Results apply to these benchmark configurations. File payloads need separate protection. See <a href=\"#/docs/footprint\">footprint &amp; build flags</a> for memory costs.</p>","text":"Benchmarks & comparisons NiFAT32 configuration: five boot copies, five FAT copies, five metadata backups; caches and indexing disabled. Throughput NiFAT32 throughput divided by the comparison filesystem's throughput. Above 1× is faster. RELATIVE THROUGHPUT Throughput by operation Compare with LittleFS FAT32 YAFFS2 LittleFS = 1× · higher is faster 0 6× 12× Open 3.48× Create 5.52× Write 5.96× Read 3.26× Rename 10.4× Copy 4.87× FAT32 = 1× · higher is faster 0 0.5× 1× Open 0.0617× Create 0.231× Write 0.14× Read 0.132× Rename 0.286× Copy 0.247× YAFFS2 = 1× · higher is faster 0 1.25× 2.5× Open 0.00829× Create 0.83× Write 2.16× Read 0.28× Rename 0.747× Copy 0.564× Exact throughput ratios Operation vs FAT32 vs LittleFS vs YAFFS2 Open 0.06173× 3.483× 0.008285× Create 0.2311× 5.525× 0.8299× Write 0.1398× 5.963× 2.165× Read 0.1323× 3.257× 0.2799× Rename 0.2856× 10.43× 0.7468× Copy 0.2465× 4.866× 0.564× Energy per operation Chart: NiFAT32 energy / comparison filesystem energy. Below 1× uses less energy. GROSS ENERGY / OPERATION Energy per operation Compare with LittleFS FAT32 YAFFS2 LittleFS = 1× · lower uses less energy 0 0.5× 1× Open 0.234× Create 0.163× Write 0.135× Read 0.25× Rename 0.0862× Copy 0.181× FAT32 = 1× · lower uses less energy 0 6× 12× Open 9.88× Create 3.76× Write 5.98× Read 6.53× Rename 2.79× Copy 3.66× YAFFS2 = 1× · lower uses less energy 0 62.5× 125× Open 122× Create 1.23× Write 0.479× Read 3.65× Rename 1.41× Copy 1.86× Exact energy ratios: comparison filesystem / NiFAT32 Operation vs FAT32 vs LittleFS vs YAFFS2 Open 0.1012× 4.28× 0.008204× Create 0.2661× 6.15× 0.8143× Write 0.1671× 7.394× 2.087× Read 0.1531× 4.007× 0.274× Rename 0.359× 11.6× 0.7106× Copy 0.2733× 5.523× 0.5368× Fault tolerance BIT-FLIP INJECTION Failed runs Image size 100 MB 200 MB 300 MB 400 MB 500 MB At 10 million injected flips 99 FAT32 14 NiFAT32 13 LittleFS + RS At 10 million injected flips 85 FAT32 0 NiFAT32 0 LittleFS + RS At 10 million injected flips 76 FAT32 0 NiFAT32 0 LittleFS + RS At 10 million injected flips 49 FAT32 0 NiFAT32 0 LittleFS + RS At 10 million injected flips 55 FAT32 0 NiFAT32 0 LittleFS + RS Failed mount or file access. Payload integrity excluded. Lower is better. LittleFS uses the ramrsbd Reed–Solomon block layer in the fault-injection test. Advantages & tradeoffs Compared with NiFAT32 advantage Tradeoff FAT32 Fewer failed runs under bit-flip injection; metadata correction and repair FAT32 is 3.50–16.2× faster in the measured operations and uses less energy. LittleFS 3.26–10.43× the measured throughput; lower gross energy per operation LittleFS + RS has slightly fewer failures in the fault-injection test. YAFFS2 2.16× write throughput; about 48% of the gross write energy YAFFS2 is faster and uses less energy for the other five operations. Results apply to these benchmark configurations. File payloads need separate protection. See footprint & build flags for memory costs.","headings":[{"id":"throughput","title":"Throughput","level":2},{"id":"energy-per-operation","title":"Energy per operation","level":2},{"id":"fault-tolerance","title":"Fault tolerance","level":2},{"id":"advantages-tradeoffs","title":"Advantages & tradeoffs","level":2}]},"docs/image":{"title":"Creating an image","group":"Build & integrate","source":"docs/image.md","html":"<h1 id=\"creating-an-image\">Creating an image</h1>\n<p>Build and run the formatter:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">make formatter\n./formatter/formatter -o nifat32.img\n</code></pre>\n<p>Create a 128 MB image and populate it from <code>data/</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">./formatter/formatter \\\n  -o nifat32.img \\\n  -s data \\\n  --volume-size 128 \\\n  --spc 8 \\\n  --fc 4 \\\n  --bsbc 5 \\\n  --b-bsbc 0 \\\n  --jc 2\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n<th>Default</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-o PATH</code></td>\n<td>Output image; existing files are overwritten</td>\n<td>Required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-s PATH</code></td>\n<td>Source directory copied recursively into the image</td>\n<td>Empty image</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--volume-size N</code></td>\n<td>Image size in MB</td>\n<td><code>64</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--spc N</code></td>\n<td>Sectors per cluster</td>\n<td><code>8</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--fc N</code></td>\n<td>FAT copy count</td>\n<td><code>4</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--bsbc N</code></td>\n<td>Boot sector copy count</td>\n<td><code>5</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--b-bsbc N</code></td>\n<td>Deliberately damaged boot sector copies for tests</td>\n<td><code>0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--jc N</code></td>\n<td>Journal sector count</td>\n<td><code>2</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The formatter creates a raw filesystem starting at sector zero, without an MBR or GPT. See <a href=\"https://github.com/j1sk1ss/NIFAT32/blob/HEAD/formatter/README.md\"><code>formatter/README.md</code></a> for SD card instructions and safety notes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"choosing-the-geometry\">Choosing the geometry</h2>\n<p>The geometry is a trade-off between redundancy and capacity:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Parameter</th>\n<th>Bigger means</th>\n<th>Cost</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--volume-size</code></td>\n<td>More user data</td>\n<td>More flash on the chip</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--fc</code></td>\n<td>More votes for every FAT entry</td>\n<td>Each extra FAT copy costs <code>2 * fat_size</code> sectors of the whole volume</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--bsbc</code></td>\n<td>More chances to find a good boot sector</td>\n<td>1 sector per copy plus the repair writes at mount</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--jc</code></td>\n<td>More votes for journal entries</td>\n<td><code>spc</code> sectors per copy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--spc</code></td>\n<td>Bigger clusters, fewer FAT entries per MB</td>\n<td>Higher internal fragmentation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The FAT copy count is the most expensive redundancy: with <code>--spc 8</code> a 64 MB volume carries a FAT of roughly 32 sectors, and each of the <code>fc</code> copies stores the Hamming-encoded table (<code>2 * fat_size</code> sectors). The <a href=\"#/builder\">Filesystem builder</a> computes all of this for any combination.</p>","text":"Creating an image Build and run the formatter: make formatter ./formatter/formatter -o nifat32.img Create a 128 MB image and populate it from data/ : ./formatter/formatter \\ -o nifat32.img \\ -s data \\ --volume-size 128 \\ --spc 8 \\ --fc 4 \\ --bsbc 5 \\ --b-bsbc 0 \\ --jc 2 Option Description Default -o PATH Output image; existing files are overwritten Required -s PATH Source directory copied recursively into the image Empty image --volume-size N Image size in MB 64 --spc N Sectors per cluster 8 --fc N FAT copy count 4 --bsbc N Boot sector copy count 5 --b-bsbc N Deliberately damaged boot sector copies for tests 0 --jc N Journal sector count 2 The formatter creates a raw filesystem starting at sector zero, without an MBR or GPT. See formatter/README.md for SD card instructions and safety notes. Choosing the geometry The geometry is a trade-off between redundancy and capacity: Parameter Bigger means Cost --volume-size More user data More flash on the chip --fc More votes for every FAT entry Each extra FAT copy costs 2 * fat_size sectors of the whole volume --bsbc More chances to find a good boot sector 1 sector per copy plus the repair writes at mount --jc More votes for journal entries spc sectors per copy --spc Bigger clusters, fewer FAT entries per MB Higher internal fragmentation The FAT copy count is the most expensive redundancy: with --spc 8 a 64 MB volume carries a FAT of roughly 32 sectors, and each of the fc copies stores the Hamming-encoded table ( 2 * fat_size sectors). The Filesystem builder computes all of this for any combination.","headings":[{"id":"choosing-the-geometry","title":"Choosing the geometry","level":2}]},"docs/initialization":{"title":"Initialization","group":"Build & integrate","source":"docs/initialization.md","html":"<h1 id=\"initialization\">Initialization</h1>\n<p>Include the public header:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">#include &quot;nifat32.h&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>NiFAT32 does not perform POSIX I/O itself. The platform supplies sector I/O, logging, and optionally memory callbacks.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">static int read_sector(\n    sector_addr_t sector,\n    sector_offset_t offset,\n    unsigned char *buffer,\n    int size\n) {\n    /* Read size bytes from sector * SECTOR_SIZE + offset. */\n    return 1;\n}\n\nstatic int write_sector(\n    sector_addr_t sector,\n    sector_offset_t offset,\n    const unsigned char *data,\n    int size\n) {\n    /* Write size bytes to sector * SECTOR_SIZE + offset. */\n    return 1;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Both callbacks return nonzero on success and zero on failure.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">#define SECTOR_SIZE 512\n#define IMAGE_SIZE_BYTES (64u * 1024u * 1024u)\n\nnifat32_params_t params = {\n    .fat_cache        = CACHE,\n    .bs_num           = 0,\n    .bs_count         = 5,\n    .ts               = IMAGE_SIZE_BYTES / SECTOR_SIZE,\n    .jc               = 2,\n    .ec               = 1,\n    .disk_io          = {\n        .read_sector  = read_sector,\n        .write_sector = write_sector,\n        .sector_size  = SECTOR_SIZE\n    },\n    .logg_io          = {\n        .fd_fprintf   = NULL,\n        .fd_vfprintf  = NULL\n    },\n    .mm_manager       = { DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER }\n};\n\nif (!NIFAT32_init(&amp;params)) {\n    /* Mount failed. */\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"mount-parameters\">Mount parameters</h2>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fat_cache</code></td>\n<td>Bit mask made from <code>NO_CACHE</code>, <code>CACHE</code>, <code>HARD_CACHE</code>, and <code>MAP_CACHE</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>bs_num</code></td>\n<td>First boot sector copy to try; normally <code>0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>bs_count</code></td>\n<td>Number of boot sector copies created by <code>--bsbc</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ts</code></td>\n<td>Total logical sectors in the image</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>jc</code></td>\n<td>Journal copy count created by <code>--jc</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ec</code></td>\n<td>Persistent error-storage copy count; use <code>0</code> to disable at runtime</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ef</code></td>\n<td>Entries offset which means it will enable presudo-random placement over the image</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>disk_io</code></td>\n<td>Sector read/write callbacks and logical sector size</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>logg_io</code></td>\n<td>Optional <code>fprintf</code>-like and <code>vfprintf</code>-like callbacks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mm_manager</code></td>\n<td>Allocator callbacks or <code>DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><code>NIFAT32_init()</code> increments <code>bs_num</code> while trying damaged boot sector copies, so the structure may be modified after a failed or recovered mount.</p>\n<p>Cache modes must be combined with <code>CACHE</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Value</th>\n<th>Behavior</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>NO_CACHE</code></td>\n<td>Read FAT values from storage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CACHE</code></td>\n<td>Allocate a lazy FAT cache</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CACHE \\| HARD_CACHE</code></td>\n<td>Load the complete FAT into the cache during mount</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CACHE \\| MAP_CACHE</code></td>\n<td>Allocate a free-cluster bitmap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CACHE \\| HARD_CACHE \\| MAP_CACHE</code></td>\n<td>Enable both full loading and the bitmap</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The image geometry and mount parameters must agree. In particular, <code>bs_count</code>, <code>ts</code>, <code>jc</code>, <code>ec</code>, and <code>sector_size</code> must describe the formatted image. FAT count and sectors per cluster are read from the boot sector.</p>\n<h2 id=\"custom-memory-manager\">Custom memory manager</h2>\n<p>The callback signatures are:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">static int mm_init(void);\nstatic void *mm_alloc(unsigned long size);\nstatic void mm_free(void *ptr);\n</code></pre>\n<p>Configure and build:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">nifat32_params_t params = {\n    /* ... */\n    .mm_manager = {\n        .init   = mm_init,\n        .malloc = mm_alloc,\n        .free   = mm_free\n    }\n};\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">make NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1\n</code></pre>\n<p>When using the built-in manager, <code>{ DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER }</code> selects its static buffer. Increase <code>ALLOC_BUFFER_SIZE</code> when FAT caching or directory indexes need more memory.</p>","text":"Initialization Include the public header: #include \"nifat32.h\" NiFAT32 does not perform POSIX I/O itself. The platform supplies sector I/O, logging, and optionally memory callbacks. static int read_sector( sector_addr_t sector, sector_offset_t offset, unsigned char *buffer, int size ) { /* Read size bytes from sector * SECTOR_SIZE + offset. */ return 1; } static int write_sector( sector_addr_t sector, sector_offset_t offset, const unsigned char *data, int size ) { /* Write size bytes to sector * SECTOR_SIZE + offset. */ return 1; } Both callbacks return nonzero on success and zero on failure. #define SECTOR_SIZE 512 #define IMAGE_SIZE_BYTES (64u * 1024u * 1024u) nifat32_params_t params = { .fat_cache = CACHE, .bs_num = 0, .bs_count = 5, .ts = IMAGE_SIZE_BYTES / SECTOR_SIZE, .jc = 2, .ec = 1, .disk_io = { .read_sector = read_sector, .write_sector = write_sector, .sector_size = SECTOR_SIZE }, .logg_io = { .fd_fprintf = NULL, .fd_vfprintf = NULL }, .mm_manager = { DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER } }; if (!NIFAT32_init(&params)) { /* Mount failed. */ } Mount parameters Field Meaning fat_cache Bit mask made from NO_CACHE , CACHE , HARD_CACHE , and MAP_CACHE bs_num First boot sector copy to try; normally 0 bs_count Number of boot sector copies created by --bsbc ts Total logical sectors in the image jc Journal copy count created by --jc ec Persistent error-storage copy count; use 0 to disable at runtime ef Entries offset which means it will enable presudo-random placement over the image disk_io Sector read/write callbacks and logical sector size logg_io Optional fprintf -like and vfprintf -like callbacks mm_manager Allocator callbacks or DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER NIFAT32_init() increments bs_num while trying damaged boot sector copies, so the structure may be modified after a failed or recovered mount. Cache modes must be combined with CACHE : Value Behavior NO_CACHE Read FAT values from storage CACHE Allocate a lazy FAT cache CACHE \\| HARD_CACHE Load the complete FAT into the cache during mount CACHE \\| MAP_CACHE Allocate a free-cluster bitmap CACHE \\| HARD_CACHE \\| MAP_CACHE Enable both full loading and the bitmap The image geometry and mount parameters must agree. In particular, bs_count , ts , jc , ec , and sector_size must describe the formatted image. FAT count and sectors per cluster are read from the boot sector. Custom memory manager The callback signatures are: static int mm_init(void); static void *mm_alloc(unsigned long size); static void mm_free(void *ptr); Configure and build: nifat32_params_t params = { /* ... */ .mm_manager = { .init = mm_init, .malloc = mm_alloc, .free = mm_free } }; make NO_DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER=1 When using the built-in manager, { DEFAULT_MM_MANAGER } selects its static buffer. Increase ALLOC_BUFFER_SIZE when FAT caching or directory indexes need more memory.","headings":[{"id":"mount-parameters","title":"Mount parameters","level":2},{"id":"custom-memory-manager","title":"Custom memory manager","level":2}]},"docs/api":{"title":"Public API","group":"Build & integrate","source":"docs/api.md","html":"<h1 id=\"public-api\">Public API</h1>\n<p>All public functions are declared in <code>nifat32.h</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"names-and-open-modes\">Names and open modes</h2>\n<p>NiFAT32 stores and accepts names in uppercase FAT 8.3 form. Convert regular names and paths before passing them to the public API.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">char path[128] = { 0 };\nnft32_path_to_fatnames(&quot;root/dir/hello.txt&quot;, path);\n/* path is now ROOT/DIR/HELLO   TXT */\n</code></pre>\n<p>Available helpers from <code>std/fatname.h</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Function</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nft32_name_to_fatname</code></td>\n<td>Convert <code>hello.txt</code> to <code>HELLO   TXT</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nft32_fatname_to_name</code></td>\n<td>Convert <code>HELLO   TXT</code> to a regular name</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nft32_path_to_fatnames</code></td>\n<td>Convert a path to the NiFAT32 path form</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nft32_extract_name</code></td>\n<td>Extract the final path component</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nft32_unpack_83_name</code></td>\n<td>Split an 11-byte FAT name into name and extension</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Open modes:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Flag</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>R_MODE</code></td>\n<td>Permit reads</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>W_MODE</code></td>\n<td>Permit writes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CR_MODE</code></td>\n<td>Create missing path components</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>NO_TARGET</code></td>\n<td>No explicit type for the final component</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>FILE_TARGET</code></td>\n<td>Create the final component as a file</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DIR_TARGET</code></td>\n<td>Create the final component as a directory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DF_MODE</code></td>\n<td><code>MODE(R_MODE \\| W_MODE, NO_TARGET)</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Use <code>MODE(access_flags, target)</code> to pack access and target flags:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">char fat_path[64] = { 0 };\nnft32_path_to_fatnames(&quot;root/hello.txt&quot;, fat_path);\n\nci_t file = NIFAT32_open_content(\n    NO_RCI,\n    fat_path,\n    MODE(R_MODE | W_MODE | CR_MODE, FILE_TARGET)\n);\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>NO_RCI</code> starts lookup at the filesystem root. Passing an open directory index instead starts lookup relative to that directory. A <code>NULL</code> path opens the root directory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-if-content-exists\">Check if content exists</h2>\n<p><code>NIFAT32_content_exists()</code> searches from the filesystem root and returns <code>1</code> when the path exists.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">char fat_path[64] = { 0 };\nnft32_path_to_fatnames(&quot;data/config.bin&quot;, fat_path);\n\nif (NIFAT32_content_exists(fat_path)) {\n  // The entry exists and can be opened.\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"open-content\">Open content</h2>\n<p>Open an entry with the required access mode. A nonnegative value is a valid content index.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">char fat_path[64] = { 0 };\nnft32_path_to_fatnames(&quot;data/config.bin&quot;, fat_path);\n\nci_t file = NIFAT32_open_content(\n    NO_RCI,\n    fat_path,\n    MODE(R_MODE | W_MODE, FILE_TARGET)\n);\n\nif (file &lt; 0) {\n  // -1 means that the content table is full.\n  // -2 means that the path was not found.\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Pass an open directory as <code>rci</code> to search relative to it:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">char directory_name[12] = { 0 };\nchar file_name[12] = { 0 };\nnft32_name_to_fatname(&quot;data&quot;, directory_name);\nnft32_name_to_fatname(&quot;config.bin&quot;, file_name);\n\nci_t directory = NIFAT32_open_content(NO_RCI, directory_name, DF_MODE);\n\nif (directory &gt;= 0) {\n  ci_t file = NIFAT32_open_content(directory, file_name, DF_MODE);\n  if (file &gt;= 0) NIFAT32_close_content(file);\n  NIFAT32_close_content(directory);\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"create-content\">Create content</h2>\n<p>Add <code>CR_MODE</code> to create missing path components. Intermediate components are created as directories.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">char fat_path[64] = { 0 };\nnft32_path_to_fatnames(&quot;logs/boot.txt&quot;, fat_path);\n\nci_t file = NIFAT32_open_content(\n    NO_RCI,\n    fat_path,\n    MODE(R_MODE | W_MODE | CR_MODE, FILE_TARGET)\n);\n\nif (file &gt;= 0) {\n  // logs/boot.txt now exists and is open.\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Use <code>NIFAT32_put_content()</code> when cluster reservation is required:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">ci_t root = NIFAT32_open_content(NO_RCI, NULL, DF_MODE);\ncinfo_t info = {\n  .full_name = &quot;LOG     BIN&quot;,\n  .type = STAT_FILE\n};\n\nif (root &gt;= 0) {\n  NIFAT32_put_content(root, &amp;info, 4); // Preallocate four clusters.\n  NIFAT32_close_content(root);\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>NO_RESERVE</code> is <code>1</code>. Creation through <code>NIFAT32_open_content()</code> is more convenient for paths, but it does not expose cluster reservation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"read-content\">Read content</h2>\n<p>The content must be opened with <code>R_MODE</code>. The function returns the number of bytes copied to the buffer.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">char buffer[64] = { 0 };\n\nint read_size = NIFAT32_read_content2buffer(\n  file,\n  0, // Byte offset in the content.\n  (buffer_t)buffer,\n  sizeof(buffer)\n);\n\nif (read_size &gt; 0) {\n  // buffer[0..read_size-1] contains the data.\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Reading a directory returns its raw encoded directory data.</p>\n<h2 id=\"write-content\">Write content</h2>\n<p>The content must be opened with <code>W_MODE</code>. The function returns the number of bytes written.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">const char message[] = &quot;System started\\n&quot;;\n\nint written = NIFAT32_write_buffer2content(\n  file,\n  0, // Byte offset in the content.\n  (const_buffer_t)message,\n  sizeof(message) - 1\n);\n\nif (written != (int)(sizeof(message) - 1)) {\n  // The complete message was not written.\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The current write implementation grows cluster chains but does not automatically update file-size metadata. Use <code>NIFAT32_change_meta()</code> or <code>NIFAT32_truncate_content()</code> when the stored size must be updated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"get-content-information\">Get content information</h2>\n<p><code>NIFAT32_stat_content()</code> fills a <code>cinfo_t</code> structure with the entry name and type.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">cinfo_t stat = { 0 };\n\nif (NIFAT32_stat_content(file, &amp;stat)) {\n  // stat.full_name contains the FAT 8.3 name.\n  // stat.type is STAT_FILE or STAT_DIR.\n}\n</code></pre>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>full_name[12]</code></td>\n<td>11-byte FAT 8.3 name plus storage for a terminator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>name[8]</code></td>\n<td>Base name</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>extention[4]</code></td>\n<td>Extension; the public field retains this spelling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>size</code></td>\n<td>File size metadata</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>type</code></td>\n<td><code>STAT_FILE</code> or <code>STAT_DIR</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h2 id=\"change-content-metadata\">Change content metadata</h2>\n<p><code>NIFAT32_change_meta()</code> changes directory entry metadata. It does not move or resize the cluster chain.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">cinfo_t info = {\n  .full_name = &quot;CONFIG  BAK&quot;,\n  .size = 128,\n  .type = STAT_FILE\n};\n\nif (!NIFAT32_change_meta(file, &amp;info)) {\n  // Metadata update failed.\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"truncate-content\">Truncate content</h2>\n<p>Truncation changes the occupied cluster chain and stored file size. The content must be open with <code>W_MODE</code>.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">if (NIFAT32_truncate_content(file, 0, 128)) {\n  // Keep 128 bytes starting from offset zero.\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"index-a-directory\">Index a directory</h2>\n<p>Index frequently searched directories to speed up repeated lookups.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">char directory_name[12] = { 0 };\nchar file_name[12] = { 0 };\nnft32_name_to_fatname(&quot;data&quot;, directory_name);\nnft32_name_to_fatname(&quot;config.bin&quot;, file_name);\n\nci_t directory = NIFAT32_open_content(NO_RCI, directory_name, DF_MODE);\n\nif (directory &gt;= 0) {\n  NIFAT32_index_content(directory);\n\n  // Relative lookups can now use the directory index.\n  ci_t file = NIFAT32_open_content(directory, file_name, DF_MODE);\n  if (file &gt;= 0) NIFAT32_close_content(file);\n  NIFAT32_close_content(directory);\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Indexing requires allocator support and has no useful effect when compiled with <code>NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"copy-content\">Copy content</h2>\n<p>The destination must already exist as a placeholder:</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Copy type</th>\n<th>Behavior</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DEEP_COPY</code></td>\n<td>Allocate a new cluster chain and recursively duplicate directory data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>SHALLOW_COPY</code></td>\n<td>Replace the destination with a link to the source cluster chain</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">char source_path[64] = { 0 };\nchar copy_path[64] = { 0 };\nnft32_path_to_fatnames(&quot;data/config.bin&quot;, source_path);\nnft32_path_to_fatnames(&quot;backup.bin&quot;, copy_path);\n\nci_t source = NIFAT32_open_content(NO_RCI, source_path, DF_MODE);\nci_t copy = NIFAT32_open_content(\n  NO_RCI,\n  copy_path,\n  MODE(R_MODE | W_MODE | CR_MODE, FILE_TARGET)\n);\n\nif (source &gt;= 0 &amp;&amp; copy &gt;= 0) {\n  NIFAT32_copy_content(source, copy, DEEP_COPY);\n}\n\nif (source &gt;= 0) NIFAT32_close_content(source);\nif (copy &gt;= 0) NIFAT32_close_content(copy);\n</code></pre>\n<p>Copying deallocates the destination's previous data. A shallow copy must not be placed in the same directory under a conflicting name.</p>\n<h2 id=\"delete-content\">Delete content</h2>\n<p>Deleting a directory also removes its children recursively. The supplied content index is closed by the function.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">char fat_path[64] = { 0 };\nnft32_path_to_fatnames(&quot;backup.bin&quot;, fat_path);\n\nci_t file = NIFAT32_open_content(NO_RCI, fat_path, DF_MODE);\n\nif (file &gt;= 0) {\n  NIFAT32_delete_content(file);\n  // Do not close file: NIFAT32_delete_content() already did it.\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"repair-boot-sectors\">Repair boot sectors</h2>\n<p><code>NIFAT32_repair_bootsectors()</code> rebuilds every boot sector copy from the currently mounted geometry.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">NIFAT32_repair_bootsectors(); // Rewrite all boot sector copies.\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"repair-content\">Repair content</h2>\n<p><code>NIFAT32_repair_content()</code> reads, corrects and rewrites directory entries. Pass `RECURSIVE_REPAIR` second argument to visit subdirectories recursively. Otherwise use `NON_RECURSIVE_REPAIR`.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">ci_t root = NIFAT32_open_content(NO_RCI, NULL, DF_MODE);\n\nif (root &gt;= 0) {\n  NIFAT32_repair_content(root, RECURSIVE_REPAIR); // Recursive repair.\n  NIFAT32_close_content(root);\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"get-filesystem-information\">Get filesystem information</h2>\n<p><code>NIFAT32_get_fs_data()</code> copies the currently mounted geometry.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">fat_data_t fs = NIFAT32_get_fs_data();\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"get-the-last-error\">Get the last error</h2>\n<p>Persistent errors are consumed from the error ring one at a time.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">error_code_t error = NIFAT32_get_last_error();\n\nif (error &gt;= 0) {\n  // Handle the registered error code.\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The function returns <code>-1</code> when no error is available. With <code>NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1</code>, registration is disabled and the function returns <code>NO_ERROR</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"close-content\">Close content</h2>\n<p>Close every successfully opened content index when it is no longer needed.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">if (file &gt;= 0) {\n  NIFAT32_close_content(file);\n}\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"unload-the-filesystem\">Unload the filesystem</h2>\n<p>Call <code>NIFAT32_unload()</code> after all work is complete. It releases the FAT cache and destroys the content table.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-c\">NIFAT32_unload();\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"read-only-builds\">Read-only builds</h2>\n<p>With <code>NIFAT32_RO=1</code>, reads, lookup, stat, indexing, and lifecycle operations remain available. Storage-mutating paths are compiled as no-ops. In the current API these no-op functions generally return success, so callers must not use the return value to infer that persistent data changed.</p>\n<p>Affected operations include write, truncate, metadata changes, creation, copy, deletion, journal writes, FAT writes, and repair writes.</p>","text":"Public API All public functions are declared in nifat32.h . Names and open modes NiFAT32 stores and accepts names in uppercase FAT 8.3 form. Convert regular names and paths before passing them to the public API. char path[128] = { 0 }; nft32_path_to_fatnames(\"root/dir/hello.txt\", path); /* path is now ROOT/DIR/HELLO TXT */ Available helpers from std/fatname.h : Function Purpose nft32_name_to_fatname Convert hello.txt to HELLO TXT nft32_fatname_to_name Convert HELLO TXT to a regular name nft32_path_to_fatnames Convert a path to the NiFAT32 path form nft32_extract_name Extract the final path component nft32_unpack_83_name Split an 11-byte FAT name into name and extension Open modes: Flag Meaning R_MODE Permit reads W_MODE Permit writes CR_MODE Create missing path components NO_TARGET No explicit type for the final component FILE_TARGET Create the final component as a file DIR_TARGET Create the final component as a directory DF_MODE MODE(R_MODE \\| W_MODE, NO_TARGET) Use MODE(access_flags, target) to pack access and target flags: char fat_path[64] = { 0 }; nft32_path_to_fatnames(\"root/hello.txt\", fat_path); ci_t file = NIFAT32_open_content( NO_RCI, fat_path, MODE(R_MODE | W_MODE | CR_MODE, FILE_TARGET) ); NO_RCI starts lookup at the filesystem root. Passing an open directory index instead starts lookup relative to that directory. A NULL path opens the root directory. Check if content exists NIFAT32_content_exists() searches from the filesystem root and returns 1 when the path exists. char fat_path[64] = { 0 }; nft32_path_to_fatnames(\"data/config.bin\", fat_path); if (NIFAT32_content_exists(fat_path)) { // The entry exists and can be opened. } Open content Open an entry with the required access mode. A nonnegative value is a valid content index. char fat_path[64] = { 0 }; nft32_path_to_fatnames(\"data/config.bin\", fat_path); ci_t file = NIFAT32_open_content( NO_RCI, fat_path, MODE(R_MODE | W_MODE, FILE_TARGET) ); if (file < 0) { // -1 means that the content table is full. // -2 means that the path was not found. } Pass an open directory as rci to search relative to it: char directory_name[12] = { 0 }; char file_name[12] = { 0 }; nft32_name_to_fatname(\"data\", directory_name); nft32_name_to_fatname(\"config.bin\", file_name); ci_t directory = NIFAT32_open_content(NO_RCI, directory_name, DF_MODE); if (directory >= 0) { ci_t file = NIFAT32_open_content(directory, file_name, DF_MODE); if (file >= 0) NIFAT32_close_content(file); NIFAT32_close_content(directory); } Create content Add CR_MODE to create missing path components. Intermediate components are created as directories. char fat_path[64] = { 0 }; nft32_path_to_fatnames(\"logs/boot.txt\", fat_path); ci_t file = NIFAT32_open_content( NO_RCI, fat_path, MODE(R_MODE | W_MODE | CR_MODE, FILE_TARGET) ); if (file >= 0) { // logs/boot.txt now exists and is open. } Use NIFAT32_put_content() when cluster reservation is required: ci_t root = NIFAT32_open_content(NO_RCI, NULL, DF_MODE); cinfo_t info = { .full_name = \"LOG BIN\", .type = STAT_FILE }; if (root >= 0) { NIFAT32_put_content(root, &info, 4); // Preallocate four clusters. NIFAT32_close_content(root); } NO_RESERVE is 1 . Creation through NIFAT32_open_content() is more convenient for paths, but it does not expose cluster reservation. Read content The content must be opened with R_MODE . The function returns the number of bytes copied to the buffer. char buffer[64] = { 0 }; int read_size = NIFAT32_read_content2buffer( file, 0, // Byte offset in the content. (buffer_t)buffer, sizeof(buffer) ); if (read_size > 0) { // buffer[0..read_size-1] contains the data. } Reading a directory returns its raw encoded directory data. Write content The content must be opened with W_MODE . The function returns the number of bytes written. const char message[] = \"System started\\n\"; int written = NIFAT32_write_buffer2content( file, 0, // Byte offset in the content. (const_buffer_t)message, sizeof(message) - 1 ); if (written != (int)(sizeof(message) - 1)) { // The complete message was not written. } The current write implementation grows cluster chains but does not automatically update file-size metadata. Use NIFAT32_change_meta() or NIFAT32_truncate_content() when the stored size must be updated. Get content information NIFAT32_stat_content() fills a cinfo_t structure with the entry name and type. cinfo_t stat = { 0 }; if (NIFAT32_stat_content(file, &stat)) { // stat.full_name contains the FAT 8.3 name. // stat.type is STAT_FILE or STAT_DIR. } Field Meaning full_name[12] 11-byte FAT 8.3 name plus storage for a terminator name[8] Base name extention[4] Extension; the public field retains this spelling size File size metadata type STAT_FILE or STAT_DIR Change content metadata NIFAT32_change_meta() changes directory entry metadata. It does not move or resize the cluster chain. cinfo_t info = { .full_name = \"CONFIG BAK\", .size = 128, .type = STAT_FILE }; if (!NIFAT32_change_meta(file, &info)) { // Metadata update failed. } Truncate content Truncation changes the occupied cluster chain and stored file size. The content must be open with W_MODE . if (NIFAT32_truncate_content(file, 0, 128)) { // Keep 128 bytes starting from offset zero. } Index a directory Index frequently searched directories to speed up repeated lookups. char directory_name[12] = { 0 }; char file_name[12] = { 0 }; nft32_name_to_fatname(\"data\", directory_name); nft32_name_to_fatname(\"config.bin\", file_name); ci_t directory = NIFAT32_open_content(NO_RCI, directory_name, DF_MODE); if (directory >= 0) { NIFAT32_index_content(directory); // Relative lookups can now use the directory index. ci_t file = NIFAT32_open_content(directory, file_name, DF_MODE); if (file >= 0) NIFAT32_close_content(file); NIFAT32_close_content(directory); } Indexing requires allocator support and has no useful effect when compiled with NIFAT32_NO_ECACHE . Copy content The destination must already exist as a placeholder: Copy type Behavior DEEP_COPY Allocate a new cluster chain and recursively duplicate directory data SHALLOW_COPY Replace the destination with a link to the source cluster chain char source_path[64] = { 0 }; char copy_path[64] = { 0 }; nft32_path_to_fatnames(\"data/config.bin\", source_path); nft32_path_to_fatnames(\"backup.bin\", copy_path); ci_t source = NIFAT32_open_content(NO_RCI, source_path, DF_MODE); ci_t copy = NIFAT32_open_content( NO_RCI, copy_path, MODE(R_MODE | W_MODE | CR_MODE, FILE_TARGET) ); if (source >= 0 && copy >= 0) { NIFAT32_copy_content(source, copy, DEEP_COPY); } if (source >= 0) NIFAT32_close_content(source); if (copy >= 0) NIFAT32_close_content(copy); Copying deallocates the destination's previous data. A shallow copy must not be placed in the same directory under a conflicting name. Delete content Deleting a directory also removes its children recursively. The supplied content index is closed by the function. char fat_path[64] = { 0 }; nft32_path_to_fatnames(\"backup.bin\", fat_path); ci_t file = NIFAT32_open_content(NO_RCI, fat_path, DF_MODE); if (file >= 0) { NIFAT32_delete_content(file); // Do not close file: NIFAT32_delete_content() already did it. } Repair boot sectors NIFAT32_repair_bootsectors() rebuilds every boot sector copy from the currently mounted geometry. NIFAT32_repair_bootsectors(); // Rewrite all boot sector copies. Repair content NIFAT32_repair_content() reads, corrects and rewrites directory entries. Pass a nonzero second argument to visit subdirectories recursively. ci_t root = NIFAT32_open_content(NO_RCI, NULL, DF_MODE); if (root >= 0) { NIFAT32_repair_content(root, 1); // Recursive repair. NIFAT32_close_content(root); } Get filesystem information NIFAT32_get_fs_data() copies the currently mounted geometry. fat_data_t fs = NIFAT32_get_fs_data(); Get the last error Persistent errors are consumed from the error ring one at a time. error_code_t error = NIFAT32_get_last_error(); if (error >= 0) { // Handle the registered error code. } The function returns -1 when no error is available. With NIFAT32_NO_ERROR=1 , registration is disabled and the function returns NO_ERROR . Close content Close every successfully opened content index when it is no longer needed. if (file >= 0) { NIFAT32_close_content(file); } Unload the filesystem Call NIFAT32_unload() after all work is complete. It releases the FAT cache and destroys the content table. NIFAT32_unload(); Read-only builds With NIFAT32_RO=1 , reads, lookup, stat, indexing, and lifecycle operations remain available. Storage-mutating paths are compiled as no-ops. In the current API these no-op functions generally return success, so callers must not use the return value to infer that persistent data changed. Affected operations include write, truncate, metadata changes, creation, copy, deletion, journal writes, FAT writes, and repair writes.","headings":[{"id":"names-and-open-modes","title":"Names and open modes","level":2},{"id":"check-if-content-exists","title":"Check if content exists","level":2},{"id":"open-content","title":"Open content","level":2},{"id":"create-content","title":"Create content","level":2},{"id":"read-content","title":"Read content","level":2},{"id":"write-content","title":"Write content","level":2},{"id":"get-content-information","title":"Get content information","level":2},{"id":"change-content-metadata","title":"Change content metadata","level":2},{"id":"truncate-content","title":"Truncate content","level":2},{"id":"index-a-directory","title":"Index a directory","level":2},{"id":"copy-content","title":"Copy content","level":2},{"id":"delete-content","title":"Delete content","level":2},{"id":"repair-boot-sectors","title":"Repair boot sectors","level":2},{"id":"repair-content","title":"Repair content","level":2},{"id":"get-filesystem-information","title":"Get filesystem information","level":2},{"id":"get-the-last-error","title":"Get the last error","level":2},{"id":"close-content","title":"Close content","level":2},{"id":"unload-the-filesystem","title":"Unload the filesystem","level":2},{"id":"read-only-builds","title":"Read-only builds","level":2}]},"docs/unix-utility":{"title":"Unix utility","group":"Build & integrate","source":"docs/unix-utility.md","html":"<h1 id=\"unix-utility\">Unix utility</h1>\n<p>The Unix utility mounts a NiFAT32 image file and exposes it through a small interactive shell. It is the same tool the integration test scenarios drive.</p>\n<p>Build:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">make unix\n</code></pre>\n<p>Run:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">./builds/unix_nifat32 nifat32.img 64 512 5 2\n</code></pre>\n<p>Arguments are image path, image size in MB, sector size, boot sector copy count, and journal count.</p>\n<p>Supported commands include <code>cd</code>, <code>ls</code>, <code>mkdir</code>, <code>mkfile</code>, <code>read</code>, <code>write</code>, <code>trunc</code>, <code>cp</code>, <code>mv</code>, <code>rm</code>, <code>frename</code>, <code>rs</code>, <code>ws</code>, and <code>get_le</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cd &lt;dir&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Change the working directory (8.3 uppercase names)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ls</code></td>\n<td>List the current directory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mkdir &lt;name&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Create a directory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mkfile &lt;name&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Create an empty file</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>read &lt;file&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Print the file content</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>write &lt;file&gt; &lt;text&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Store text in the file (first token only)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>trunc &lt;file&gt; &lt;size&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Truncate the file</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cp &lt;src&gt; &lt;dst&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Copy a file (shallow)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mv &lt;src&gt; &lt;dst&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Move/rename a file</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rm &lt;file&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Delete a file</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>frename &lt;old&gt; &lt;new&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Rename a file</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rs</code></td>\n<td>Read all boot sector copies (debug)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ws</code></td>\n<td>Write all boot sector copies (debug)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>get_le</code></td>\n<td>Drain the persistent error ring (debug)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The prompt is <code>&lt;NAME&gt; &gt;</code> without a trailing newline, and paths are 8.3 uppercase, so <code>write</code> stores only its first token and <code>read</code> prints until the first NUL byte. Those quirks are what the integration scenarios assert against.</p>","text":"Unix utility The Unix utility mounts a NiFAT32 image file and exposes it through a small interactive shell. It is the same tool the integration test scenarios drive. Build: make unix Run: ./builds/unix_nifat32 nifat32.img 64 512 5 2 Arguments are image path, image size in MB, sector size, boot sector copy count, and journal count. Supported commands include cd , ls , mkdir , mkfile , read , write , trunc , cp , mv , rm , frename , rs , ws , and get_le . Command Purpose cd <dir> Change the working directory (8.3 uppercase names) ls List the current directory mkdir <name> Create a directory mkfile <name> Create an empty file read <file> Print the file content write <file> <text> Store text in the file (first token only) trunc <file> <size> Truncate the file cp <src> <dst> Copy a file (shallow) mv <src> <dst> Move/rename a file rm <file> Delete a file frename <old> <new> Rename a file rs Read all boot sector copies (debug) ws Write all boot sector copies (debug) get_le Drain the persistent error ring (debug) The prompt is <NAME> > without a trailing newline, and paths are 8.3 uppercase, so write stores only its first token and read prints until the first NUL byte. Those quirks are what the integration scenarios assert against.","headings":[]},"docs/testing":{"title":"Testing","group":"Build & integrate","source":"docs/testing.md","html":"<h1 id=\"testing\">Testing</h1>\n<p>Four test systems run from the root Makefile. Each system builds the tools it needs and keeps images and logs under its own folder, which is git-ignored.</p>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable reference table\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Target</th>\n<th>System</th>\n<th>Runs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>make unit</code></td>\n<td><code>utests/</code></td>\n<td>C unit tests of library tools plus single-filesystem runs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>make integration</code></td>\n<td><code>itests/</code></td>\n<td>Declarative scenarios driven through the <code>unix_nifat32</code> shell</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>make bench</code></td>\n<td><code>bench/</code></td>\n<td>C benchmark scenarios with parameter sweeps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>make robust</code></td>\n<td><code>robust/</code></td>\n<td>SEU/MEU robustness smoke loops</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>make robust-full</code></td>\n<td><code>robust/</code></td>\n<td>Smoke loops plus bitflip scenario campaigns</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Aggregated targets:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">make test      # unit + integration\nmake test-all  # unit + integration + bench + robust-full\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"unit\">Unit</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">make unit\n</code></pre>\n<p>The target builds the unit test binary, formats a fresh 16 MB image (spc 8, fc 5, bsbc 5, jc 0, ec 0) into <code>builds/utests/</code>, and runs every test registered in <code>utests/</code>. Helper tests run against an in-memory fake disk, and the <code>fs_*</code> runs mount the pristine image through the public API. A new <code>utests/test_*.c</code> file is picked up by the build automatically.</p>\n<pre><code>utests: 175 assertions, 0 failed\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"integration\">Integration</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">make integration\nmake integration INTEG_ARGS=&quot;--scenario basic_crud&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>A scenario is a declarative <code>.scn</code> file in <code>itests/scenarios/</code>: <code>basic_crud</code>, <code>copy_move</code>, <code>error_paths</code>, <code>nested_dirs</code>, <code>rename_trunc</code>. The runner formats an image, starts <code>unix_nifat32</code>, replays the commands, and checks the output with <code>expect:</code> and <code>expect-not:</code> lines. Per-scenario logs go to <code>itests/results/</code>, work images to <code>itests/work/</code>, which <code>INTEG_ARGS=\"--keep\"</code> preserves.</p>\n<h2 id=\"benchmarks\">Benchmarks</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">make bench\nmake bench BENCH_ARGS=&quot;--scenario cold_boot&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>A scenario is a <code>.bench</code> file in <code>bench/scenarios/</code>: <code>cold_boot</code>, <code>different_bs</code>, <code>fat_ops</code>, <code>many_files</code>, <code>sweep_fat</code>, <code>throughput</code>. The <code>@test</code>, <code>@runs</code>, and <code>@sweep</code> directives pick the C benchmark, the run count, and the parameter sweep. The runner compiles the benchmark, runs it on a fresh image, and stores the measured values under <code>bench/results/&lt;timestamp&gt;/&lt;name&gt;/</code>. <code>bench/collector.sh</code> sweeps the standalone runner over <code>--fat-count</code>, <code>--bs-count</code>, or <code>--b-bs-count</code> and writes the series to <code>collected_results/</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"robustness\">Robustness</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">make robust\nmake robust ROBUST_ARGS=&quot;--scenario seu_basic&quot;\nmake robust-full\n</code></pre>\n<p>Smoke scenarios in <code>robust/scenarios/</code> (<code>seu_basic</code>, <code>seu_stress</code>, <code>meu_mixed</code>, <code>meu_scratch</code>) set up an image, inject single-event or multiple-event upsets, and verify the filesystem, reporting the failure rate per loop. <code>robust-full</code> additionally runs the bitflip scenario campaigns from <code>robust/campaigns/</code> (<code>seu_campaign</code>, <code>meu_campaign</code>) through <code>test/nifat32_tests.py</code>. Campaign steps use the <code>T/I/S/C</code> grammar: <code>T</code> is a forward test, <code>I</code> is random single-bit flip injection, <code>S</code> is a scratch upset, and <code>C</code> restores the clean snapshot.</p>\n<h2 id=\"standalone-c-test-runner\">Standalone C test runner</h2>\n<p>The test runner can build a fresh image and execute the C test suite:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">python3 test/nifat32_tests.py \\\n  --new-image \\\n  --formatter formatter \\\n  --tests-folder test \\\n  --root-folder . \\\n  --clean \\\n  --test-type default\n</code></pre>\n<p>Fault-injection tests:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">python3 test/nifat32_tests.py \\\n  --new-image \\\n  --formatter formatter \\\n  --tests-folder test \\\n  --root-folder . \\\n  --clean \\\n  --test-type bitflip \\\n  --injector-scenario test/injector_scenario.txt\n</code></pre>\n<p>See <a href=\"https://github.com/j1sk1ss/NIFAT32/blob/HEAD/test/README.md\"><code>test/README.md</code></a> for campaign and collector options.</p>","text":"Testing Four test systems run from the root Makefile. Each system builds the tools it needs and keeps images and logs under its own folder, which is git-ignored. Target System Runs make unit utests/ C unit tests of library tools plus single-filesystem runs make integration itests/ Declarative scenarios driven through the unix_nifat32 shell make bench bench/ C benchmark scenarios with parameter sweeps make robust robust/ SEU/MEU robustness smoke loops make robust-full robust/ Smoke loops plus bitflip scenario campaigns Aggregated targets: make test # unit + integration make test-all # unit + integration + bench + robust-full Unit make unit The target builds the unit test binary, formats a fresh 16 MB image (spc 8, fc 5, bsbc 5, jc 0, ec 0) into builds/utests/ , and runs every test registered in utests/ . Helper tests run against an in-memory fake disk, and the fs_* runs mount the pristine image through the public API. A new utests/test_*.c file is picked up by the build automatically. utests: 175 assertions, 0 failed Integration make integration make integration INTEG_ARGS=\"--scenario basic_crud\" A scenario is a declarative .scn file in itests/scenarios/ : basic_crud , copy_move , error_paths , nested_dirs , rename_trunc . The runner formats an image, starts unix_nifat32 , replays the commands, and checks the output with expect: and expect-not: lines. Per-scenario logs go to itests/results/ , work images to itests/work/ , which INTEG_ARGS=\"--keep\" preserves. Benchmarks make bench make bench BENCH_ARGS=\"--scenario cold_boot\" A scenario is a .bench file in bench/scenarios/ : cold_boot , different_bs , fat_ops , many_files , sweep_fat , throughput . The @test , @runs , and @sweep directives pick the C benchmark, the run count, and the parameter sweep. The runner compiles the benchmark, runs it on a fresh image, and stores the measured values under bench/results/<timestamp>/<name>/ . bench/collector.sh sweeps the standalone runner over --fat-count , --bs-count , or --b-bs-count and writes the series to collected_results/ . Robustness make robust make robust ROBUST_ARGS=\"--scenario seu_basic\" make robust-full Smoke scenarios in robust/scenarios/ ( seu_basic , seu_stress , meu_mixed , meu_scratch ) set up an image, inject single-event or multiple-event upsets, and verify the filesystem, reporting the failure rate per loop. robust-full additionally runs the bitflip scenario campaigns from robust/campaigns/ ( seu_campaign , meu_campaign ) through test/nifat32_tests.py . Campaign steps use the T/I/S/C grammar: T is a forward test, I is random single-bit flip injection, S is a scratch upset, and C restores the clean snapshot. Standalone C test runner The test runner can build a fresh image and execute the C test suite: python3 test/nifat32_tests.py \\ --new-image \\ --formatter formatter \\ --tests-folder test \\ --root-folder . \\ --clean \\ --test-type default Fault-injection tests: python3 test/nifat32_tests.py \\ --new-image \\ --formatter formatter \\ --tests-folder test \\ --root-folder . \\ --clean \\ --test-type bitflip \\ --injector-scenario test/injector_scenario.txt See test/README.md for campaign and collector options.","headings":[{"id":"unit","title":"Unit","level":2},{"id":"integration","title":"Integration","level":2},{"id":"benchmarks","title":"Benchmarks","level":2},{"id":"robustness","title":"Robustness","level":2},{"id":"standalone-c-test-runner","title":"Standalone C test runner","level":2}]},"animations":{"title":"How it works","group":"Interactive tools","source":"animations.md","html":"<h1 id=\"how-it-works\">How it works</h1>\n<p>Follow the bytes through NiFAT32. Step through each operation, play it end to end, or inject a fault and watch the protection layers respond.</p>\n<p><a class=\"text-link tool-link\" href=\"pages/animations.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open walkthroughs in a full window</a></p>\n<iframe class=\"embed\" src=\"pages/animations.html\" title=\"Interactive NiFAT32 layout, mount, write, and upset recovery walkthroughs\"></iframe>\n\n<h2 id=\"explore-the-four-scenes\">Explore the four scenes</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Layout</strong> — see where boot copies, FAT copies, journals, and error areas land, and where the data clusters live.</li>\n<li><strong>Mount</strong> — follow boot sector fallback, repair, geometry readout, and journal replay.</li>\n<li><strong>Write</strong> — trace allocation, write-ahead logging, directory updates, and FAT replication.</li>\n<li><strong>Upset &amp; repair</strong> — try real Hamming(15,11) decoding and majority voting. A single flipped bit is corrected during decoding; a double-flipped copy can lose the vote. Corrupting a majority can defeat recovery.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Use <strong>Previous</strong>, <strong>Play / Pause</strong>, and <strong>Next</strong> to control each scene. In the upset scene, inject a fault first, then step through read, decode, vote, and repair.</p>\n<p>Read the <a href=\"#/docs/architecture\">architecture guide</a> for the algorithms behind the visualization.</p>","text":"How it works Follow the bytes through NiFAT32. Step through each operation, play it end to end, or inject a fault and watch the protection layers respond. Open walkthroughs in a full window Explore the four scenes Layout — see where boot copies, FAT copies, journals, and error areas land, and where the data clusters live. Mount — follow boot sector fallback, repair, geometry readout, and journal replay. Write — trace allocation, write-ahead logging, directory updates, and FAT replication. Upset & repair — try real Hamming(15,11) decoding and majority voting. A single flipped bit is corrected during decoding; a double-flipped copy can lose the vote. Corrupting a majority can defeat recovery. Use Previous , Play / Pause , and Next to control each scene. In the upset scene, inject a fault first, then step through read, decode, vote, and repair. Read the architecture guide for the algorithms behind the visualization.","headings":[{"id":"explore-the-four-scenes","title":"Explore the four scenes","level":2}]},"builder":{"title":"Filesystem builder","group":"Interactive tools","source":"builder.md","html":"<h1 id=\"filesystem-builder\">Filesystem builder</h1>\n<p>Place NiFAT32 on your flash, inspect the separation between redundant copies, and move a localized particle beam across the memory map.</p>\n<p><a class=\"text-link tool-link\" href=\"pages/builder.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open builder in a full window</a></p>\n<iframe class=\"embed\" src=\"pages/builder.html\" title=\"NiFAT32 square flash map, particle beam, and filesystem builder\"></iframe>\n\n<h2 id=\"square-flash-map\">Square flash map</h2>\n<p>The square chip view highlights every boot, FAT, journal, and error-storage copy at the flash offsets calculated by the formatter. Choose a copy to highlight all of its mapped tiles; hover over a tile to inspect its address range. The current volume offset is included, so moving the volume moves its copies on the chip.</p>\n<p>STM32 and ATmega presets show <strong>internal program flash</strong>. ESP32 presets show the <strong>external flash memory</strong>, rather than placing storage inside the ESP32 processor. The preset selector groups these three families first.</p>\n<p>The default view maps ascending addresses to rows, with an optional serpentine order. These are <strong>spatial models</strong>, not measured physical die layouts. Known erase sizes define the tile subdivision, but an address range alone does not establish physical distance on silicon. Square package outlines are schematic. On STM32 presets, outlines group tiles into the unequal erase sectors; ATmega tiles aggregate two 256-byte pages into one 512-byte NiFAT32 sector.</p>\n<p>For device-specific coordinates, open <strong>Use measured address-to-position coordinates</strong> and import a JSON map. The map must identify the chip, flash size, coordinate units, source, array dimensions, and a physical rectangle for each address range. Export the current model for a complete editable example. Imported coordinates are identified as user-supplied; exported grid models retain their model label.</p>\n<h2 id=\"move-the-particle-beam\">Move the particle beam</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Drag the <strong>circular beam spot</strong> across the memory array, or position it with the X/Y sliders. Arrow keys move the beam; Shift moves it faster.</li>\n<li>Change the radius, or switch to a <strong>particle track</strong> and set its angle. Radius and track half-width are percentages of array width; imported maps in <code>um</code> or <code>mm</code> also show the corresponding coordinate distance.</li>\n<li>Inspect the intersected-copy count separately for FAT, boot, journal, and error storage. <strong>Center on a FAT copy</strong> gives an immediate example; <strong>Hide beam</strong> clears the overlay.</li>\n<li>Choose a structure family to isolate its copies, or select an individual copy below the map to reveal its entire footprint.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An intersection exposes the <strong>whole mapped address range</strong>. It does not claim that every cell flips or that a copy becomes unreadable. FAT and journal voting is per entry: touching multiple copies need not damage the same entry in each. An untouched majority is a conservative indication of remaining redundancy; its absence is not proof of data loss.</p>\n<h2 id=\"volume-and-device-configuration\">Volume and device configuration</h2>\n<p>Configure flash offset, volume size, sectors per cluster, and the numbers of FAT copies, boot copies, journals, and error-storage copies. The linear address map remains available under <strong>Linear address map &amp; partitions</strong>; the sector map supports zoom and pan.</p>\n<p>Validation checks volume bounds, occupied partition overlap, metadata collisions, and allocator RAM. Preset partitions are examples, not a partition table read from your board. The builder estimates ROM/RAM from host measurements; see <a href=\"#/docs/footprint\">reference footprint</a> for the build configurations and <a href=\"#/docs/image\">creating an image</a> for formatter commands.</p>\n<h2 id=\"device-references\">Device references</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.st.com/resource/en/reference_manual/rm0090-stm32f4xx-reference-manual-stmicroelectronics.pdf\">STMicroelectronics RM0090</a>: STM32F407 erase-sector organization.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.espressif.com/projects/esp-idf/en/stable/esp32/api-guides/partition-tables.html\">Espressif partition tables</a>: flash-relative partition addresses.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://ww1.microchip.com/downloads/en/devicedoc/atmega640-1280-1281-2560-2561-datasheet-ds40002211a.pdf\">Microchip ATmega640/1280/1281/2560/2561 datasheet</a>, section 30.5: ATmega2560 flash pages are 128 words (256 bytes); EEPROM is a separate 4 KiB memory.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.winbond.com/resource-files/W25Q32JV%20RevJ%2012242024%20Plus.pdf\">Winbond W25Q32JV datasheet</a>: example external flash organization.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These references support address and erase geometry. No measured address-to-die coordinate map is bundled with the presets.</p>","text":"Filesystem builder Place NiFAT32 on your flash, inspect the separation between redundant copies, and move a localized particle beam across the memory map. Open builder in a full window Square flash map The square chip view highlights every boot, FAT, journal, and error-storage copy at the flash offsets calculated by the formatter. Choose a copy to highlight all of its mapped tiles; hover over a tile to inspect its address range. The current volume offset is included, so moving the volume moves its copies on the chip. STM32 and ATmega presets show internal program flash . ESP32 presets show the external flash memory , rather than placing storage inside the ESP32 processor. The preset selector groups these three families first. The default view maps ascending addresses to rows, with an optional serpentine order. These are spatial models , not measured physical die layouts. Known erase sizes define the tile subdivision, but an address range alone does not establish physical distance on silicon. Square package outlines are schematic. On STM32 presets, outlines group tiles into the unequal erase sectors; ATmega tiles aggregate two 256-byte pages into one 512-byte NiFAT32 sector. For device-specific coordinates, open Use measured address-to-position coordinates and import a JSON map. The map must identify the chip, flash size, coordinate units, source, array dimensions, and a physical rectangle for each address range. Export the current model for a complete editable example. Imported coordinates are identified as user-supplied; exported grid models retain their model label. Move the particle beam Drag the circular beam spot across the memory array, or position it with the X/Y sliders. Arrow keys move the beam; Shift moves it faster. Change the radius, or switch to a particle track and set its angle. Radius and track half-width are percentages of array width; imported maps in um or mm also show the corresponding coordinate distance. Inspect the intersected-copy count separately for FAT, boot, journal, and error storage. Center on a FAT copy gives an immediate example; Hide beam clears the overlay. Choose a structure family to isolate its copies, or select an individual copy below the map to reveal its entire footprint. An intersection exposes the whole mapped address range . It does not claim that every cell flips or that a copy becomes unreadable. FAT and journal voting is per entry: touching multiple copies need not damage the same entry in each. An untouched majority is a conservative indication of remaining redundancy; its absence is not proof of data loss. Volume and device configuration Configure flash offset, volume size, sectors per cluster, and the numbers of FAT copies, boot copies, journals, and error-storage copies. The linear address map remains available under Linear address map & partitions ; the sector map supports zoom and pan. Validation checks volume bounds, occupied partition overlap, metadata collisions, and allocator RAM. Preset partitions are examples, not a partition table read from your board. The builder estimates ROM/RAM from host measurements; see reference footprint for the build configurations and creating an image for formatter commands. Device references STMicroelectronics RM0090 : STM32F407 erase-sector organization. Espressif partition tables : flash-relative partition addresses. Microchip ATmega640/1280/1281/2560/2561 datasheet , section 30.5: ATmega2560 flash pages are 128 words (256 bytes); EEPROM is a separate 4 KiB memory. Winbond W25Q32JV datasheet : example external flash organization. These references support address and erase geometry. No measured address-to-die coordinate map is bundled with the presets.","headings":[{"id":"square-flash-map","title":"Square flash map","level":2},{"id":"move-the-particle-beam","title":"Move the particle beam","level":2},{"id":"volume-and-device-configuration","title":"Volume and device configuration","level":2},{"id":"device-references","title":"Device references","level":2}]}};
